{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SgokXZUKPPCK8SkKJfAKPu","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (57.1KB). Full output saved to: /Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-06-providers-and-role-resolution/682f1424-ed5e-4ad8-82df-9c53c97a2745/tool-results/bttdkazba.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n# Area 02: Project config and built-in providers\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nThis area owns the **project config** (`.roast.json`) and the registry of\n**built-in providers**. It:\n\n1. Finds the project config nearest the working directory, searching\n   upward no further than the Git toplevel.\n2. Parses it as JSONC and validates every field (one zod schema is the\n   source of truth; `roast.schema.json` is generated from it).\n3. Applies defaults and normalizes values (profile protocol, output\n   limit, author-route family names, confidence floors).\n4. Resolves a **role** (finder or verifier) into a runtime-ready role:\n   the **inference profile**'s model, endpoint, protocol, output limit,\n   session header, and the **credential** picked from environment\n   variables or a **stored credential**.\n5. Re-binds the credential when command-line flags move a role to another\n   endpoint or protocol.\n6. Selects an alternate finder profile from the author's model family\n   (author routes) and validates a resolved role before any model call.\n7. Knows each built-in provider's endpoint, accepted protocols, standard\n   key variable, key page, and login method.\n\nOut of scope, referenced only: the trust projection and its hash (area\n\"trust and credentials\"), the stored-credential file format and lookup\norder within the store (same area), the `init` starter config and model\nplacement (area \"doctor and init\"), CLI flag parsing and the use of limits\nduring a review (area \"CLI\").\n\n## 2. Behavior contract\n\n### 2.1 Constants\n\n| Name | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Config file name | `.roast.json` |\n| Supported `version` | `1` |\n| Default `max_output_tokens` | `32768` |\n| Default `review.confidence_floor` | `0.6` |\n| Default `review.quick_confidence_floor` | `0.8` |\n| OpenAI default endpoint | `https://api.openai.com/v1` |\n| Anthropic default endpoint | `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` |\n| OpenRouter endpoint | `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1` |\n| Max `session_header` length | 64 (characters; all are ASCII) |\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"682f1424-ed5e-4ad8-82df-9c53c97a2745","uuid":"58ed942d-14a2-4c23-acf9-0d9e11ea37d9","timestamp":"2026-11-18T20:09:48.550Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Area 02: Project config and built-in providers\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nThis area owns the **project config** (`.roast.json`) and the registry of\n**built-in providers**. It:\n\n1. Finds the project config nearest the working directory, searching\n   upward no further than the Git toplevel.\n2. Parses it as JSONC and validates every field (one zod schema is the\n   source of truth; `roast.schema.json` is generated from it).\n3. Applies defaults and normalizes values (profile protocol, output\n   limit, author-route family names, confidence floors).\n4. Resolves a **role** (finder or verifier) into a runtime-ready role:\n   the **inference profile**'s model, endpoint, protocol, output limit,\n   session header, and the **credential** picked from environment\n   variables or a **stored credential**.\n5. Re-binds the credential when command-line flags move a role to another\n   endpoint or protocol.\n6. Selects an alternate finder profile from the author's model family\n   (author routes) and validates a resolved role before any model call.\n7. Knows each built-in provider's endpoint, accepted protocols, standard\n   key variable, key page, and login method.\n\nOut of scope, referenced only: the trust projection and its hash (area\n\"trust and credentials\"), the stored-credential file format and lookup\norder within the store (same area), the `init` starter config and model\nplacement (area \"doctor and init\"), CLI flag parsing and the use of limits\nduring a review (area \"CLI\").\n\n## 2. Behavior contract\n\n### 2.1 Constants\n\n| Name | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Config file name | `.roast.json` |\n| Supported `version` | `1` |\n| Default `max_output_tokens` | `32768` |\n| Default `review.confidence_floor` | `0.6` |\n| Default `review.quick_confidence_floor` | `0.8` |\n| OpenAI default endpoint | `https://api.openai.com/v1` |\n| Anthropic default endpoint | `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` |\n| OpenRouter endpoint | `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1` |\n| Max `session_header` length | 64 (characters; all are ASCII) |\n| Default profile protocol | `responses` |\n\nThe defaults that the CLI applies to unset `limits`, `interactive`, and\n`gate` settings live in the CLI and gate areas. For reference (they must\nmatch the descriptions in the generated schema):\n\n| Setting | Default | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `limits.deadline` | `10m` | `0s` disables |\n| `limits.request_timeout` | `10m` | |\n| `limits.max_total_tokens` | `3000000` | `0` disables |\n| `limits.max_diff_bytes` | `1000000` | `200000` for `--quick` |\n| `limits.max_prompt_bytes` | `8000000` | |\n| `limits.max_file_ctx_bytes` | `400000` | `100000` for `--quick` |\n| `limits.max_attempts` | `4` | includes the first attempt |\n| `limits.max_verified` | `25` | |\n| `interactive.min_files` | `4` | |\n| `interactive.min_diff_lines` | `80` | |\n| `gate.timeout_seconds` | `180` | `0` disables |\n| `gate.severity` | `HIGH` | compared upper-cased |\n| `gate.offer` | `true` | |\n| `review.snapshot` | `true` | |\n\nA config value overrides the default; a command-line flag (where one\nexists) overrides the config for one invocation (`flag -> config ->\ndefault`). The quick-mode defaults apply only when the config leaves the\nlimit unset.\n\n### 2.2 Config discovery\n\n`findConfig(start, stop)` returns the path of the nearest `.roast.json` at\nor above `start`, never looking above `stop`, or `\"\"` when there is none.\n`stop` must be `start` or an ancestor of it.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n1. `dir = path.resolve(start)` (absolute, symlinks NOT resolved).\n2. `stopCanonical = realpath(path.resolve(stop))`. A failure (for example\n   `stop` does not exist) is an error `find project config: <cause>`.\n3. Loop:\n   1. `candidate = join(dir, \".roast.json\")`. `stat` it (following\n      symlinks). If it exists and is not a directory, return `candidate`\n      (spelled through the non-canonical `dir`). A directory named\n      `.roast.json` is skipped. A stat error other than \"not found\" (for\n      example permission denied) is an error\n      `find project config: <cause>`.\n   2. `canonical = realpath(dir)`; failure is an error as above.\n   3. If `canonical === stopCanonical`, or `dirname(dir) === dir` (the\n      filesystem root), return `\"\"`.\n   4. `dir = dirname(dir)`.\n\nThe comparison uses canonical paths because on macOS a temp directory is\n`/var/...` while `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` prints `/private/var/...`.\nIf `stop` is not an ancestor, the walk ends at the filesystem root.\n\nCLI entry points (module `src/cli/config.ts` in the CLI area, specified\nhere because they are config behavior):\n\n- `projectConfigPath(explicit)`: if `explicit` (the `--config` flag value)\n  is non-empty, return it unchanged (no existence check; `loadConfig`\n  reports a missing file). Otherwise `cwd = process.cwd()` (an error is\n  `find project config: <cause>`), `stop` = Git toplevel of `cwd`\n  (`git -C <cwd> rev-parse --show-toplevel`, trimmed); if that fails\n  (not a repository, or `git` missing), `stop = cwd`. Return\n  `findConfig(cwd, stop)`.\n- `gateConfigPath(repo)`: `findConfig(repo, repo)`. The pre-commit hook\n  runs from the Git toplevel without `--config`, so only a toplevel\n  `.roast.json` counts.\n- `loadProjectConfig(explicit)`: path from `projectConfigPath`; any error\n  is a usage error (exit 2) with the error's message. An empty path is the\n  usage error \"no project config (.roast.json) found between here and the\n  Git toplevel; run `npx roast init` to create one\". A `loadConfig` error\n  is a usage error with its message. On success, attach the stored\n  credential store for the working directory (see 2.6) and return.\n- Usage error for `gate enable` with no toplevel config: \"the pre-commit\n  gate runs from the Git toplevel and finds no .roast.json there; put the\n  project config at the toplevel (`npx roast init`) before enabling the\n  gate\". (Used by the gate area.)\n- No user-level config is ever read (no `~/.config/roast/config.*`); with\n  no project config, a review is a usage error naming `npx roast init`\n  and sends no request.\n\n### 2.3 Loading and parsing\n\n`loadConfig(path)`:\n\n1. Read the file as UTF-8. Failure: `open config <path>: <cause>`.\n2. Decode (below). Failure: `parse config <path>: <cause>`.\n3. Record `dir = path.resolve(dirname(path))` on the config (used by\n   `promptPath`).\n4. Normalize and validate (2.4). These errors are returned WITHOUT the\n   `parse config <path>:` prefix (Go returns them bare).\n\nDecoding, in this order (the first failure wins):\n\n1. Parse JSONC: `//` line comments, `/* */` block comments, and trailing\n   commas in objects and arrays are allowed (the `init` starter writes a\n   trailing comma). Anything else that is not\n   JSON is an error (YAML, unquoted keys, single quotes, empty input).\n2. The document must be exactly one JSON value; trailing content after it\n   (for example a second object) is an error (\"config must contain\n   exactly one JSON object\").\n3. Version header: the value must be an object (an array, string, or\n   number is a type error). Read `version`:\n   - absent or `null`: error `version is required (set \"version\": 1)`.\n   - not an integer number (for example `\"1\"`): type error.\n   - an integer other than 1 (`0`, `2`): error `unsupported config\n     version <n> (this build supports version 1)`.\n4. Literal credential: if `inference_profiles` is an object, and any of\n   its values is an object containing the key `api_key` (any value,\n   including `null`), error `inference_profiles.<name>.api_key is not\n   allowed: a project config never holds a credential; name its\n   environment variable with api_key_env`. This check runs before the\n   unknown-field check so it gives the clearer message, and it applies\n   even when `api_key_env` is also set. If `inference_profiles` is not an\n   object of objects, this step is skipped and step 5 reports the type\n   error.\n5. Strict structural decode against the schema (2.4): unknown keys at any\n   object level are errors naming the key (for example `upload`,\n   `gateway`, `base_url`, `unknown_limit`); every type mismatch is an\n   error.\n\nNull handling (Go `encoding/json` parity): a JSON `null` for any field\nmeans \"unset\" -- the field takes its zero/unset value exactly as if it\nwere absent (`\"limits\": null`, `\"max_output_tokens\": null`,\n`\"snapshot\": null`, `\"pin\": null`, a profile value of `null` are all\naccepted). The exception is `version` (step 3), where `null` is \"required\".\n\nDuplicate keys: the last occurrence wins (same as Go `encoding/json`).\n\nKey case: JSON keys match exactly, case-sensitively (deliberate change 7\nin `spec.md`). Go matched struct keys case-insensitively; here `\"Version\"`,\n`\"INFERENCE_PROFILES\"`, and `\"Model\"` are unknown fields and rejected.\n\nInteger lexemes: an integer field rejects a number whose raw token text is\nnot a plain integer (`1.0`, `1e3`), as Go does (\"Config numbers\" in\n`spec.md`). The check uses the raw token text, not the parsed value.\n\nLone surrogates: validation rejects any string (key or value) containing a\nlone UTF-16 surrogate, so the trust hash is always defined.\n\n### 2.4 Fields of `.roast.json`\n\nTop level (object, unknown keys rejected):\n\n| Key | Type | Required | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `$schema` | string | no | Editor schema reference; ignored. |\n| `version` | integer | yes | Must be `1`. |\n| `inference_profiles` | object of profile | no | Named profiles. |\n| `roles` | object of role | no | `finder` and/or `verifier`. |\n| `review` | object | no | Review defaults. |\n| `prompts` | object | no | Prompt-override paths. |\n| `limits` | object | no | Per-review limits. |\n| `interactive` | object | no | Interactive-mode thresholds. |\n| `gate` | object | no | Pre-commit gate settings. |\n\n`{\"version\": 1}` alone is a valid config. There are no built-in roles or\nmodels: a role absent from `roles` fails only when it is resolved.\n\n#### Inference profile (`inference_profiles.<name>`)\n\nUnknown keys rejected. Every string field treats `\"\"` as unset.\n\n| Key | Type | Default | Validation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `endpoint` | string | protocol default (2.7) | see endpoint rule |\n| `api_key_env` | string | unset | `^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$` |\n| `model` | string | `\"\"` | none at load; checked by `validateRole` |\n| `protocol` | string | `responses` | `chat`, `responses`, `anthropic` |\n| `max_output_tokens` | integer | `32768` | `0`/unset -> default; `< 0` error |\n| `session_header` | string | unset | see session-header rule |\n\nEvery profile is normalized and validated whether or not a role uses it,\nin document order (Go iterates a map, so with several bad profiles the\nreported one is arbitrary; TS reports the first in document order):\n\n1. Name: `name.trim() === \"\"` is an error `inference profile names cannot\n   be empty`.\n2. `protocol` empty -> `responses`. `max_output_tokens` `0` -> `32768`.\n3. `api_key_env` set and not matching the pattern: error\n   `inference_profiles.<name>.api_key_env is not a valid environment\n   variable name`.\n4. Endpoint rule, applied to the effective endpoint (`endpoint`, or the\n   protocol default when unset), with `field = inference_profiles.<name>`\n   (shared with `validateRole`, see 2.9, `validateEndpoint`):\n   - The URL must parse, scheme `http` or `https`, non-empty host, no\n     userinfo (`user:pass@`), no query, no fragment. Else\n     `<field> endpoint must be an http(s) URL without credentials, a\n     query, or a fragment`.\n   - Protocol must be `chat`, `responses`, or `anthropic`. Else\n     `<field> protocol must be chat, responses, or anthropic`.\n   - `max_output_tokens <= 0` (after defaulting, so only a negative\n     value): `<field> max_output_tokens must be positive`.\n5. Session-header rule (only when non-empty), field\n   `inference_profiles.<name>.session_header`:\n   - At most 64 characters and every character an RFC 9110 token\n     character: ASCII letters, digits, or one of\n     ``!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~``. Else `<field> must be an HTTP header name of at\n     most 64 letters, digits, or !#$%&'*+-.^_`|~ characters`.\n   - Not reserved, compared case-insensitively: any name starting with\n     `x-stainless-`, or one of `Authorization`, `X-Api-Key`,\n     `Proxy-Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Host`, `Content-Type`,\n     `Content-Length`, `Content-Encoding`, `Transfer-Encoding`,\n     `Connection`, `Keep-Alive`, `Proxy-Connection`, `TE`, `Trailer`,\n     `Upgrade`, `Expect`, `Accept`, `Accept-Encoding`, `User-Agent`,\n     `X-Agent-Session-Id`, `Anthropic-Version`, `Anthropic-Beta`,\n     `Anthropic-Workspace-Id`, `OpenAI-Organization`, `OpenAI-Project`.\n     Else `<field> cannot be \"<name>\", which is reserved for credentials,\n     request framing, or a header Roast or the provider SDK sets`.\n   - The value is kept as written (case preserved).\n\n#### Role (`roles.<name>`)\n\nKeys of `roles` must be `finder` or `verifier` (case-sensitive); any other\nkey is an error `roles.<name> is not a role (finder or verifier)`. Role\nobject, unknown keys rejected:\n\n| Key | Type | Default | Validation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `profile` | string | required | non-empty; must name a profile |\n| `pin` | boolean | `false` | finder only in effect |\n| `author_routes` | object of string | `{}` | see below |\n\nErrors, in this order per role (roles in document order):\n\n1. `profile` empty or absent: `roles.<name>.profile is required`.\n2. Unknown profile: `roles.<name>.profile references unknown inference\n   profile \"<profile>\"`.\n3. For each `author_routes` entry (document order): normalize the key as\n   `canonicalFamily(key.trim().toLowerCase())` (`gemini` -> `google`).\n   - Normalized key empty: `roles.<name>.author_routes contains empty\n     model family`.\n   - Value not a profile name: `roles.<name>.author_routes.<key>\n     references unknown inference profile \"<value>\"` (`<key>` is the\n     normalized key).\n   - Two keys normalizing to the same value: `roles.<name>.author_routes\n     contains duplicate family \"<key>\"`.\n\n`pin` and `author_routes` are accepted on `verifier` too but have no\neffect there (only finder selection reads them).\n\nKeys of `author_routes` can be either an exact author model ID\n(lower-cased) or a family name; see 2.10.\n\n#### `review`\n\n| Key | Type | Default | Validation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `confidence_floor` | number | `0.6` | `0..1`; `0` -> default |\n| `quick_confidence_floor` | number | `0.8` | `0..1`; `0` -> default |\n| `excludes` | array of string | `[]` | none |\n| `snapshot` | boolean | `true` | none |\n\nAn explicit `0` floor cannot be expressed: it becomes the default. Both\nfloors are validated to `0..1` (deliberate change 20); a value outside the\nrange is an error `<key> must be between 0 and 1 (got <value>)`, checked\nin `normalize` before the floor defaults apply. `excludes` are added\nbefore any `--exclude` flags.\n\n#### `prompts`\n\n| Key | Type | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `finder_code` | string | Finder prompt for code changes |\n| `finder_prose` | string | Finder prompt for prose changes |\n| `verifier` | string | Verifier prompt |\n\n`\"\"` is unset (the embedded template is used). The raw strings are part\nof the trust projection (trust area). Callers read the file through\n`promptPath` (2.8), never the raw string. Contents are used verbatim.\n\n#### `limits`\n\nEvery key is optional; unset means the CLI default (2.1). Optional numbers\ndistinguish an explicit `0` from unset.\n\n| Key | Type | Minimum |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `deadline` | duration string | `>= 0` |\n| `request_timeout` | duration string | `>= 0` |\n| `max_total_tokens` | integer | `0` |\n| `max_diff_bytes` | integer | `1` |\n| `max_prompt_bytes` | integer | `1` |\n| `max_file_ctx_bytes` | integer | `1` |\n| `max_attempts` | integer | `1` |\n| `max_verified` | integer | `0` |\n\n#### `interactive`\n\n| Key | Type | Minimum |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `min_files` | integer | `0` |\n| `min_diff_lines` | integer | `0` |\n\n#### `gate`\n\n| Key | Type | Validation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `timeout_seconds` | integer | `>= 0` |\n| `severity` | string | `INFO`, `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `CRITICAL`, any case |\n| `offer` | boolean | none (`\"no\"` is a type error) |\n\n`severity` is stored as written; consumers upper-case it. `\"\"` is unset.\n\n#### Settings validation order and messages\n\nAfter profiles and roles, defaults for the two confidence floors are\napplied, then settings are checked in this fixed order (first failure\nwins):\n\n1. Integers below their minimum, in the order `limits.max_total_tokens`,\n   `limits.max_diff_bytes`, `limits.max_prompt_bytes`,\n   `limits.max_file_ctx_bytes`, `limits.max_attempts`,\n   `limits.max_verified`, `interactive.min_files`,\n   `interactive.min_diff_lines`, `gate.timeout_seconds`:\n   `<key> must be an integer >= <min> (got <value>)`.\n2. Negative durations, `limits.deadline` then `limits.request_timeout`:\n   `<key> cannot be negative`.\n3. `gate.severity`: `gate.severity must be INFO, LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, or\n   CRITICAL (got \"<value>\")`.\n\nInteger fields reject non-integers (`1.5`), non-integer lexemes (`1.0`,\n`1e3`), and non-numbers as type errors during the structural decode.\n\n#### Duration strings\n\nA duration is a JSON string in Go `time.ParseDuration` syntax, parsed by\nthe shared helper `parseGoDuration` (this module owns it; the CLI area\nuses it for flags):\n\n- Grammar: optional sign `+` or `-`, then one or more `<number><unit>`\n  groups, where `<number>` is digits with an optional fraction (`1`,\n  `1.5`, `.5`, `1.`; at least one digit overall) and `<unit>` is `ns`,\n  `us`, `µs` (U+00B5), `μs` (U+03BC), `ms`, `s`, `m`, `h`. The bare string\n  `0` (optionally signed) is also valid. A missing unit (`\"10\"`) is\n  invalid. Results that overflow a signed 64-bit nanosecond count are\n  invalid.\n- A non-string value: error `duration must be a string such as \"90s\" (got\n  <raw JSON>)`.\n- An unparseable string: error `invalid duration \"<value>\"`.\n- A negative result is rejected by settings validation (`-1s`), but\n  `-0s` is zero and accepted.\n- Represent the value as integer milliseconds or a `bigint` of\n  nanoseconds; Go keeps nanoseconds. Sub-millisecond precision only\n  matters for equality with Go in tests.\n\n### 2.5 Role resolution\n\n`resolveRole(cfg, name, ctx)` expands a role into a `ResolvedRole`:\n\n1. If `roles[name]` is absent or its profile is empty: error `role \"<name>\"\n   is not configured`.\n2. `resolveProfile(name, profileName, selection)`:\n   - Unknown profile: `role <name> references unknown inference profile\n     \"<profile>\"` (unreachable after load validation except via author\n     routes).\n   - Fields: `profile` (name), `baseURL` = effective endpoint,\n     `explicitEndpoint` = profile set `endpoint`, `model`, `protocol`,\n     `maxOutputTokens`, `sessionHeader`, `pin` and `authorRoutes` from the\n     selection, and the attached stored-credential store.\n   - Credential from `resolveCredential` (2.6). An error is wrapped as\n     `role <name>: <cause>`.\n\nOnly the credential comes from outside the config. In particular these\nenvironment variables have NO effect: `ROAST_<ROLE>_BASE_URL`,\n`ROAST_<ROLE>_PROTOCOL`, `ROAST_<ROLE>_MODEL`,\n`ROAST_<ROLE>_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS`, `OPENAI_BASE_URL`, `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL`,\nand any author-model variable.\n\n`ResolvedRole.apiKey` is secret: it must never be copied into run\nmetadata, logs, debug output, or error messages.\n\n### 2.6 Credential resolution\n\n`<ROLE>` below is `name.toUpperCase()`; the role variable is\n`ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`. An environment variable counts only when its value\nis non-empty.\n\n`resolveCredential(roleName, profile, role)`:\n\n1. Role variable `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY` set -> use it,\n   source `{kind: 'role_env', env: 'ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY'}`.\n2. Profile `api_key_env` named:\n   1. That variable set -> use it, source `{kind: 'profile_env', env}`.\n   2. Else, `ambientCredential(role.protocol, role.baseURL, stored)`.\n      If it returns a key, use it with the ambient source. If it errors,\n      return the error. If it returns no key, return no key with source\n      `{kind: 'profile_env', env: <api_key_env>}` (no `withheld`).\n3. Otherwise return `ambientCredential(role.protocol, role.baseURL,\n   stored)`.\n\n`ambientCredential(protocol, endpoint, stored)`:\n\n1. `provider = providerAt(endpoint, protocol)` (2.7). If found:\n   1. `env[provider.keyEnv]` set -> use it, source\n      `{kind: 'provider_env', env: provider.keyEnv}`.\n   2. `storedCredential(stored, protocol, endpoint)`: if it errors,\n      return the error; if it returns a key, use it.\n2. Return no key with source `{kind: 'none', withheld:\n   withheldKey(protocol)}`.\n\n`storedCredential(stored, protocol, endpoint)`: if `providerAt` finds no\nprovider, or no store is attached, return none. Else\n`stored.lookup(provider.name)`; an error is returned as is; an empty key\nis none; else source `{kind: 'stored', provider: provider.name, scope}`.\nThe registry alone decides the provider, so a custom endpoint never\nreceives a stored key.\n\n`withheldKey(protocol)`: candidates, in order, are the standard key\nvariable of the provider at `defaultBaseURL(protocol)` (`OPENAI_API_KEY`\nfor `responses`/`chat` and any unknown protocol, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for\n`anthropic`), then every registry provider's `keyEnv` in registry order\n(`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`). Return the\nfirst one that is set, else `\"\"`. Because it is called only after no key\nwas sent, any variable it names was withheld.\n\nResulting precedence (for the default store):\n\n1. `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`\n2. the profile's `api_key_env`\n3. the built-in provider's standard variable (only on that provider's\n   endpoint with a protocol it accepts there)\n4. the provider's project-scope stored credential (same condition)\n5. the provider's user-scope stored credential (same condition)\n\nSteps 4 and 5 are inside the store (`lookup` returns project before\nuser); see the trust-and-credentials area.\n\nStored-credential interface (implemented by the credentials module, in\nthe trust-and-credentials area):\n\n```ts\nexport interface StoredCredentials {\n    // '' key when none is stored. Rejects (throws) when a credentials\n    // file cannot be used; it never skips a bad file.\n    lookup(provider:string):{key:string, scope:'project'|'user'|''}\n}\n```\n\nThe store is read lazily: a broken credentials file fails resolution only\nwhen a role actually reaches step 4 (no environment credential first).\nThe CLI attaches `credentials.newStore('.')` to every config it loads\n(`withStoredCredentials`); any new code path that resolves roles from a\nloaded config must attach it too.\n\n`CredentialSource`:\n\n```ts\ntype CredentialKind =\n    'role_env'|'profile_env'|'provider_env'|'stored'|'none'\ninterface CredentialSource {\n    kind:CredentialKind\n    env?:string       // variable read, for env kinds\n    provider?:string  // stored: provider name\n    scope?:string     // stored: 'project' or 'user'\n    withheld?:string  // none: a set provider key that was not sent\n}\n```\n\nIts human-readable form (`describeSource`), never containing a secret:\n\n| Kind | Text |\n| --- | --- |\n| `role_env` | `<env> (role override)` |\n| `profile_env` | `<env> (profile api_key_env)` |\n| `provider_env` | `<env> (ambient provider key)` |\n| `stored` | `stored <scope> credential for <provider>` |\n| `none`, withheld | see below |\n| `none` | `none` |\n\nThe withheld form is `none; <withheld> is set but is sent only to its\nprovider's default endpoint`.\n\n`credentialEnvNames(cfg)`: every non-empty `api_key_env` across all\nprofiles (duplicates allowed), so run-record capture can always redact\nthem (record area).\n\n### 2.7 Built-in providers\n\nRegistry, in this order (order matters for `withheldKey` and listings):\n\n1. `openai`\n   - endpoint `https://api.openai.com/v1`\n   - protocols `responses`, `chat` (first = the one starter profiles use)\n   - keyEnv `OPENAI_API_KEY`\n   - keyPage `https://platform.openai.com/api-keys`\n   - login `paste`\n2. `anthropic`\n   - endpoint `https://api.anthropic.com/v1`\n   - protocols `anthropic`\n   - keyEnv `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`\n   - keyPage `https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys`\n   - login `paste`\n3. `openrouter`\n   - endpoint `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1`\n   - protocols `chat`, `responses`\n   - keyEnv `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`\n   - keyPage `https://openrouter.ai/settings/keys`\n   - login `browser`\n\nLogin methods: `paste` opens the key page and reads a pasted key;\n`browser` runs a browser key exchange (OpenRouter PKCE, login area).\n\nInvariant: no two entries share an (endpoint, protocol) pair, so\n`providerAt` finds at most one.\n\n- `builtinProviders()`: copies of the registry (callers cannot mutate\n  it).\n- `lookupProvider(name)`: the provider with exactly this name\n  (case-sensitive), or undefined.\n- `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)`: the first provider that accepts\n  `protocol` and whose endpoint `sameEndpoint`s `endpoint`, or undefined.\n- `defaultBaseURL(protocol)`: `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` for\n  `anthropic`, otherwise `https://api.openai.com/v1` (including unknown\n  protocols). OpenRouter is never a protocol's implicit default. A\n  profile that omits `endpoint` uses this.\n\n`sameEndpoint(a, b)`: trim both and parse as URLs; if either fails to\nparse or has an empty host, false. Otherwise equal when scheme matches\ncase-insensitively, host (including port) matches case-insensitively,\npaths match after stripping trailing `/` characters, raw query strings\nmatch exactly, and userinfo matches exactly.\n\nExamples (`providerAt`): `https://api.openai.com/v1/` + `chat` ->\nopenai; `HTTPS://API.OPENAI.COM/v1` + `responses` -> openai;\n`https://api.openai.com/v1` + `anthropic` -> none;\n`https://api.anthropic.com/v1` + `chat` -> none; OpenRouter + `responses`\n-> openrouter; OpenRouter + `anthropic` -> none; `http://api.openai.com/v1`\n-> none; `https://api.openai.com/v2` -> none; `\"\"` -> none.\n\nConsumers: trust standardness (trust area: a profile on a built-in\nprovider's endpoint, with an accepted protocol and the standard key\nvariable, needs no approval), ambient credentials (2.6), the `init`\nstarter (doctor/init area), `login`/`logout` provider lookup and\n`providersNeedingLogin` (login area), and the credentials file's provider\nvalidation. Adding a provider means adding a registry row only.\n\n### 2.8 Prompt paths\n\n`promptPath(cfg, p)`: `\"\"` stays `\"\"`; an absolute path is returned as\nis; if the config has no recorded `dir` (not loaded from a file) the path\nis returned as is; otherwise `path.join(cfg.dir, p)` (normalized). So the\nsame file is read wherever in the repository Roast runs. The trust\nprojection hashes the raw string, so approvals survive worktree moves.\n\n### 2.9 Endpoint overrides (`--<role>-base-url`, `--<role>-protocol`)\n\n`overrideEndpoint(roleName, role, baseURL?, protocol?)` applies the\noverrides, then re-binds the credential. `undefined` means the flag was\nnot given (an explicitly given empty string is still an override). It\nfails only when a stored credential the role now needs cannot be read.\nThe CLI calls it only when at least one of the two flags was given, then\napplies `--<role>-model` and `--<role>-max-output-tokens` directly.\n\n`applyEndpointOverrides(role, baseURL?, protocol?)` returns\n`crossedProvider`:\n\n1. `crossed = false`.\n2. If `protocol` given: `previous = defaultBaseURL(role.protocol)`;\n   `role.protocol = protocol`; `next = defaultBaseURL(protocol)`. If\n   `next !== previous`:\n   - if `!role.explicitEndpoint`: `role.baseURL = next`, `crossed = true`;\n   - else if `sameEndpoint(role.baseURL, previous)`: `role.baseURL =\n     next`, `crossed = true` (`explicitEndpoint` stays true);\n   - else the explicit custom endpoint stays.\n3. If `baseURL` given: `role.baseURL = baseURL`, `explicitEndpoint =\n   true`, `crossed = false`.\n\n`rebindCredential(roleName, role, crossed)`:\n\n1. `role_env`: keep the credential.\n2. `profile_env` and not `crossed`: keep it (even when its key is empty).\n   A profile credential follows an explicit endpoint override.\n3. Otherwise: if `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY` is set, use it (`role_env`).\n   Else `ambientCredential(role.protocol, role.baseURL, stored)`; an\n   error becomes `role <name>: <cause>`.\n\nConsequences (pinned by tests):\n\nEach item reads \"start; override -> endpoint, key\". \"Implicit\" means\nthe profile sets no `endpoint`; \"profile key\" is the `api_key_env` value.\n\n1. implicit, `api_key_env` set; protocol `anthropic` -> Anthropic\n   default, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`.\n2. explicit `https://api.openai.com/v1/`, `api_key_env`; protocol\n   `anthropic` -> Anthropic default, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`.\n3. explicit custom endpoint, `api_key_env`; protocol `anthropic` ->\n   unchanged endpoint, profile key.\n4. implicit, `api_key_env`; protocol `chat` -> OpenAI default, profile\n   key.\n5. implicit, `api_key_env`; protocol `anthropic` and a base URL -> the\n   chosen URL, profile key.\n6. OpenRouter chat with `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`; base URL -> chosen URL, no\n   key.\n7. OpenRouter chat; protocol `responses` -> OpenRouter,\n   `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`.\n8. OpenRouter chat; protocol `anthropic` -> OpenRouter, no key.\n9. implicit with `OPENAI_API_KEY`; base URL -> chosen URL, no key.\n10. `anthropic` protocol, only an anthropic stored key; custom base URL\n    -> no key.\n11. implicit `anthropic`, only an anthropic stored key; protocol\n    `responses` -> OpenAI default, no key.\n12. explicit Anthropic default, only an anthropic stored key; protocol\n    `chat` -> OpenAI default, no key.\n13. implicit `anthropic`, stored keys for every provider; protocol\n    `responses` -> the project-scope OpenAI stored key.\n14. OpenRouter chat, unset `api_key_env` value, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` set;\n    protocol `responses` -> same key before and after.\n\nInitial resolution (`resolveCredential`) and re-binding must agree: both\nreach the provider variable and the stored key only through\n`ambientCredential`, so a role picks the same credential with or without\na same-provider protocol override.\n\n### 2.10 Model families and finder flip\n\n`inferFamily(model)`: provider-neutral family, or `\"\"`.\n\n1. `m = model.trim().toLowerCase()`; empty -> `\"\"`.\n2. If `m` contains `/`: split at the first `/`. If the prefix is one of\n   `anthropic`, `openai`, `google`, `gemini`, `mistral`, `xai`, `cohere`","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-06-providers-and-role-resolution/682f1424-ed5e-4ad8-82df-9c53c97a2745/tool-results/bttdkazba.txt","persistedOutputSize":58425},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SgokXZUKPPCK8SkKJfAKPu","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChAzxv8bWurcqF3AY8DYE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P9ifTZm3gfDYjihBenHPrD","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-06-providers-and-role-resolution/682f1424-ed5e-4ad8-82df-9c53c97a2745/tool-results/bttdkazba.txt","allow_large":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":929,"cache_read_input_tokens":60960,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":929},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"682f1424-ed5e-4ad8-82df-9c53c97a2745","uuid":"fc53d0ed-5f9d-4aff-b3cf-8655c3d017fe","timestamp":"2026-11-18T20:09:50.447Z","request_id":"req_011ChAzxuvT5Tvy2Cz7VFv55","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01P9ifTZm3gfDYjihBenHPrD":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-06-providers-and-role-resolution/682f1424-ed5e-4ad8-82df-9c53c97a2745/tool-results/bttdkazba.txt","allow_large":true}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P9ifTZm3gfDYjihBenHPrD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Area 02: Project config and built-in providers\n2\t\n3\t## 1. Purpose\n4\t\n5\tThis area owns the **project config** (`.roast.json`) and the registry of\n6\t**built-in providers**. It:\n7\t\n8\t1. Finds the project config nearest the working directory, searching\n9\t   upward no further than the Git toplevel.\n10\t2. Parses it as JSONC and validates every field (one zod schema is the\n11\t   source of truth; `roast.schema.json` is generated from it).\n12\t3. Applies defaults and normalizes values (profile protocol, output\n13\t   limit, author-route family names, confidence floors).\n14\t4. Resolves a **role** (finder or verifier) into a runtime-ready role:\n15\t   the **inference profile**'s model, endpoint, protocol, output limit,\n16\t   session header, and the **credential** picked from environment\n17\t   variables or a **stored credential**.\n18\t5. Re-binds the credential when command-line flags move a role to another\n19\t   endpoint or protocol.\n20\t6. Selects an alternate finder profile from the author's model family\n21\t   (author routes) and validates a resolved role before any model call.\n22\t7. Knows each built-in provider's endpoint, accepted protocols, standard\n23\t   key variable, key page, and login method.\n24\t\n25\tOut of scope, referenced only: the trust projection and its hash (area\n26\t\"trust and credentials\"), the stored-credential file format and lookup\n27\torder within the store (same area), the `init` starter config and model\n28\tplacement (area \"doctor and init\"), CLI flag parsing and the use of limits\n29\tduring a review (area \"CLI\").\n30\t\n31\t## 2. Behavior contract\n32\t\n33\t### 2.1 Constants\n34\t\n35\t| Name | Value |\n36\t| --- | --- |\n37\t| Config file name | `.roast.json` |\n38\t| Supported `version` | `1` |\n39\t| Default `max_output_tokens` | `32768` |\n40\t| Default `review.confidence_floor` | `0.6` |\n41\t| Default `review.quick_confidence_floor` | `0.8` |\n42\t| OpenAI default endpoint | `https://api.openai.com/v1` |\n43\t| Anthropic default endpoint | `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` |\n44\t| OpenRouter endpoint | `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1` |\n45\t| Max `session_header` length | 64 (characters; all are ASCII) |\n46\t| Default profile protocol | `responses` |\n47\t\n48\tThe defaults that the CLI applies to unset `limits`, `interactive`, and\n49\t`gate` settings live in the CLI and gate areas. For reference (they must\n50\tmatch the descriptions in the generated schema):\n51\t\n52\t| Setting | Default | Notes |\n53\t| --- | --- | --- |\n54\t| `limits.deadline` | `10m` | `0s` disables |\n55\t| `limits.request_timeout` | `10m` | |\n56\t| `limits.max_total_tokens` | `3000000` | `0` disables |\n57\t| `limits.max_diff_bytes` | `1000000` | `200000` for `--quick` |\n58\t| `limits.max_prompt_bytes` | `8000000` | |\n59\t| `limits.max_file_ctx_bytes` | `400000` | `100000` for `--quick` |\n60\t| `limits.max_attempts` | `4` | includes the first attempt |\n61\t| `limits.max_verified` | `25` | |\n62\t| `interactive.min_files` | `4` | |\n63\t| `interactive.min_diff_lines` | `80` | |\n64\t| `gate.timeout_seconds` | `180` | `0` disables |\n65\t| `gate.severity` | `HIGH` | compared upper-cased |\n66\t| `gate.offer` | `true` | |\n67\t| `review.snapshot` | `true` | |\n68\t\n69\tA config value overrides the default; a command-line flag (where one\n70\texists) overrides the config for one invocation (`flag -> config ->\n71\tdefault`). The quick-mode defaults apply only when the config leaves the\n72\tlimit unset.\n73\t\n74\t### 2.2 Config discovery\n75\t\n76\t`findConfig(start, stop)` returns the path of the nearest `.roast.json` at\n77\tor above `start`, never looking above `stop`, or `\"\"` when there is none.\n78\t`stop` must be `start` or an ancestor of it.\n79\t\n80\tAlgorithm:\n81\t\n82\t1. `dir = path.resolve(start)` (absolute, symlinks NOT resolved).\n83\t2. `stopCanonical = realpath(path.resolve(stop))`. A failure (for example\n84\t   `stop` does not exist) is an error `find project config: <cause>`.\n85\t3. Loop:\n86\t   1. `candidate = join(dir, \".roast.json\")`. `stat` it (following\n87\t      symlinks). If it exists and is not a directory, return `candidate`\n88\t      (spelled through the non-canonical `dir`). A directory named\n89\t      `.roast.json` is skipped. A stat error other than \"not found\" (for\n90\t      example permission denied) is an error\n91\t      `find project config: <cause>`.\n92\t   2. `canonical = realpath(dir)`; failure is an error as above.\n93\t   3. If `canonical === stopCanonical`, or `dirname(dir) === dir` (the\n94\t      filesystem root), return `\"\"`.\n95\t   4. `dir = dirname(dir)`.\n96\t\n97\tThe comparison uses canonical paths because on macOS a temp directory is\n98\t`/var/...` while `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` prints `/private/var/...`.\n99\tIf `stop` is not an ancestor, the walk ends at the filesystem root.\n100\t\n101\tCLI entry points (module `src/cli/config.ts` in the CLI area, specified\n102\there because they are config behavior):\n103\t\n104\t- `projectConfigPath(explicit)`: if `explicit` (the `--config` flag value)\n105\t  is non-empty, return it unchanged (no existence check; `loadConfig`\n106\t  reports a missing file). Otherwise `cwd = process.cwd()` (an error is\n107\t  `find project config: <cause>`), `stop` = Git toplevel of `cwd`\n108\t  (`git -C <cwd> rev-parse --show-toplevel`, trimmed); if that fails\n109\t  (not a repository, or `git` missing), `stop = cwd`. Return\n110\t  `findConfig(cwd, stop)`.\n111\t- `gateConfigPath(repo)`: `findConfig(repo, repo)`. The pre-commit hook\n112\t  runs from the Git toplevel without `--config`, so only a toplevel\n113\t  `.roast.json` counts.\n114\t- `loadProjectConfig(explicit)`: path from `projectConfigPath`; any error\n115\t  is a usage error (exit 2) with the error's message. An empty path is the\n116\t  usage error \"no project config (.roast.json) found between here and the\n117\t  Git toplevel; run `npx roast init` to create one\". A `loadConfig` error\n118\t  is a usage error with its message. On success, attach the stored\n119\t  credential store for the working directory (see 2.6) and return.\n120\t- Usage error for `gate enable` with no toplevel config: \"the pre-commit\n121\t  gate runs from the Git toplevel and finds no .roast.json there; put the\n122\t  project config at the toplevel (`npx roast init`) before enabling the\n123\t  gate\". (Used by the gate area.)\n124\t- No user-level config is ever read (no `~/.config/roast/config.*`); with\n125\t  no project config, a review is a usage error naming `npx roast init`\n126\t  and sends no request.\n127\t\n128\t### 2.3 Loading and parsing\n129\t\n130\t`loadConfig(path)`:\n131\t\n132\t1. Read the file as UTF-8. Failure: `open config <path>: <cause>`.\n133\t2. Decode (below). Failure: `parse config <path>: <cause>`.\n134\t3. Record `dir = path.resolve(dirname(path))` on the config (used by\n135\t   `promptPath`).\n136\t4. Normalize and validate (2.4). These errors are returned WITHOUT the\n137\t   `parse config <path>:` prefix (Go returns them bare).\n138\t\n139\tDecoding, in this order (the first failure wins):\n140\t\n141\t1. Parse JSONC: `//` line comments, `/* */` block comments, and trailing\n142\t   commas in objects and arrays are allowed (the `init` starter writes a\n143\t   trailing comma). Anything else that is not\n144\t   JSON is an error (YAML, unquoted keys, single quotes, empty input).\n145\t2. The document must be exactly one JSON value; trailing content after it\n146\t   (for example a second object) is an error (\"config must contain\n147\t   exactly one JSON object\").\n148\t3. Version header: the value must be an object (an array, string, or\n149\t   number is a type error). Read `version`:\n150\t   - absent or `null`: error `version is required (set \"version\": 1)`.\n151\t   - not an integer number (for example `\"1\"`): type error.\n152\t   - an integer other than 1 (`0`, `2`): error `unsupported config\n153\t     version <n> (this build supports version 1)`.\n154\t4. Literal credential: if `inference_profiles` is an object, and any of\n155\t   its values is an object containing the key `api_key` (any value,\n156\t   including `null`), error `inference_profiles.<name>.api_key is not\n157\t   allowed: a project config never holds a credential; name its\n158\t   environment variable with api_key_env`. This check runs before the\n159\t   unknown-field check so it gives the clearer message, and it applies\n160\t   even when `api_key_env` is also set. If `inference_profiles` is not an\n161\t   object of objects, this step is skipped and step 5 reports the type\n162\t   error.\n163\t5. Strict structural decode against the schema (2.4): unknown keys at any\n164\t   object level are errors naming the key (for example `upload`,\n165\t   `gateway`, `base_url`, `unknown_limit`); every type mismatch is an\n166\t   error.\n167\t\n168\tNull handling (Go `encoding/json` parity): a JSON `null` for any field\n169\tmeans \"unset\" -- the field takes its zero/unset value exactly as if it\n170\twere absent (`\"limits\": null`, `\"max_output_tokens\": null`,\n171\t`\"snapshot\": null`, `\"pin\": null`, a profile value of `null` are all\n172\taccepted). The exception is `version` (step 3), where `null` is \"required\".\n173\t\n174\tDuplicate keys: the last occurrence wins (same as Go `encoding/json`).\n175\t\n176\tKey case: JSON keys match exactly, case-sensitively (deliberate change 7\n177\tin `spec.md`). Go matched struct keys case-insensitively; here `\"Version\"`,\n178\t`\"INFERENCE_PROFILES\"`, and `\"Model\"` are unknown fields and rejected.\n179\t\n180\tInteger lexemes: an integer field rejects a number whose raw token text is\n181\tnot a plain integer (`1.0`, `1e3`), as Go does (\"Config numbers\" in\n182\t`spec.md`). The check uses the raw token text, not the parsed value.\n183\t\n184\tLone surrogates: validation rejects any string (key or value) containing a\n185\tlone UTF-16 surrogate, so the trust hash is always defined.\n186\t\n187\t### 2.4 Fields of `.roast.json`\n188\t\n189\tTop level (object, unknown keys rejected):\n190\t\n191\t| Key | Type | Required | Meaning |\n192\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n193\t| `$schema` | string | no | Editor schema reference; ignored. |\n194\t| `version` | integer | yes | Must be `1`. |\n195\t| `inference_profiles` | object of profile | no | Named profiles. |\n196\t| `roles` | object of role | no | `finder` and/or `verifier`. |\n197\t| `review` | object | no | Review defaults. |\n198\t| `prompts` | object | no | Prompt-override paths. |\n199\t| `limits` | object | no | Per-review limits. |\n200\t| `interactive` | object | no | Interactive-mode thresholds. |\n201\t| `gate` | object | no | Pre-commit gate settings. |\n202\t\n203\t`{\"version\": 1}` alone is a valid config. There are no built-in roles or\n204\tmodels: a role absent from `roles` fails only when it is resolved.\n205\t\n206\t#### Inference profile (`inference_profiles.<name>`)\n207\t\n208\tUnknown keys rejected. Every string field treats `\"\"` as unset.\n209\t\n210\t| Key | Type | Default | Validation |\n211\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n212\t| `endpoint` | string | protocol default (2.7) | see endpoint rule |\n213\t| `api_key_env` | string | unset | `^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$` |\n214\t| `model` | string | `\"\"` | none at load; checked by `validateRole` |\n215\t| `protocol` | string | `responses` | `chat`, `responses`, `anthropic` |\n216\t| `max_output_tokens` | integer | `32768` | `0`/unset -> default; `< 0` error |\n217\t| `session_header` | string | unset | see session-header rule |\n218\t\n219\tEvery profile is normalized and validated whether or not a role uses it,\n220\tin document order (Go iterates a map, so with several bad profiles the\n221\treported one is arbitrary; TS reports the first in document order):\n222\t\n223\t1. Name: `name.trim() === \"\"` is an error `inference profile names cannot\n224\t   be empty`.\n225\t2. `protocol` empty -> `responses`. `max_output_tokens` `0` -> `32768`.\n226\t3. `api_key_env` set and not matching the pattern: error\n227\t   `inference_profiles.<name>.api_key_env is not a valid environment\n228\t   variable name`.\n229\t4. Endpoint rule, applied to the effective endpoint (`endpoint`, or the\n230\t   protocol default when unset), with `field = inference_profiles.<name>`\n231\t   (shared with `validateRole`, see 2.9, `validateEndpoint`):\n232\t   - The URL must parse, scheme `http` or `https`, non-empty host, no\n233\t     userinfo (`user:pass@`), no query, no fragment. Else\n234\t     `<field> endpoint must be an http(s) URL without credentials, a\n235\t     query, or a fragment`.\n236\t   - Protocol must be `chat`, `responses`, or `anthropic`. Else\n237\t     `<field> protocol must be chat, responses, or anthropic`.\n238\t   - `max_output_tokens <= 0` (after defaulting, so only a negative\n239\t     value): `<field> max_output_tokens must be positive`.\n240\t5. Session-header rule (only when non-empty), field\n241\t   `inference_profiles.<name>.session_header`:\n242\t   - At most 64 characters and every character an RFC 9110 token\n243\t     character: ASCII letters, digits, or one of\n244\t     ``!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~``. Else `<field> must be an HTTP header name of at\n245\t     most 64 letters, digits, or !#$%&'*+-.^_`|~ characters`.\n246\t   - Not reserved, compared case-insensitively: any name starting with\n247\t     `x-stainless-`, or one of `Authorization`, `X-Api-Key`,\n248\t     `Proxy-Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Host`, `Content-Type`,\n249\t     `Content-Length`, `Content-Encoding`, `Transfer-Encoding`,\n250\t     `Connection`, `Keep-Alive`, `Proxy-Connection`, `TE`, `Trailer`,\n251\t     `Upgrade`, `Expect`, `Accept`, `Accept-Encoding`, `User-Agent`,\n252\t     `X-Agent-Session-Id`, `Anthropic-Version`, `Anthropic-Beta`,\n253\t     `Anthropic-Workspace-Id`, `OpenAI-Organization`, `OpenAI-Project`.\n254\t     Else `<field> cannot be \"<name>\", which is reserved for credentials,\n255\t     request framing, or a header Roast or the provider SDK sets`.\n256\t   - The value is kept as written (case preserved).\n257\t\n258\t#### Role (`roles.<name>`)\n259\t\n260\tKeys of `roles` must be `finder` or `verifier` (case-sensitive); any other\n261\tkey is an error `roles.<name> is not a role (finder or verifier)`. Role\n262\tobject, unknown keys rejected:\n263\t\n264\t| Key | Type | Default | Validation |\n265\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n266\t| `profile` | string | required | non-empty; must name a profile |\n267\t| `pin` | boolean | `false` | finder only in effect |\n268\t| `author_routes` | object of string | `{}` | see below |\n269\t\n270\tErrors, in this order per role (roles in document order):\n271\t\n272\t1. `profile` empty or absent: `roles.<name>.profile is required`.\n273\t2. Unknown profile: `roles.<name>.profile references unknown inference\n274\t   profile \"<profile>\"`.\n275\t3. For each `author_routes` entry (document order): normalize the key as\n276\t   `canonicalFamily(key.trim().toLowerCase())` (`gemini` -> `google`).\n277\t   - Normalized key empty: `roles.<name>.author_routes contains empty\n278\t     model family`.\n279\t   - Value not a profile name: `roles.<name>.author_routes.<key>\n280\t     references unknown inference profile \"<value>\"` (`<key>` is the\n281\t     normalized key).\n282\t   - Two keys normalizing to the same value: `roles.<name>.author_routes\n283\t     contains duplicate family \"<key>\"`.\n284\t\n285\t`pin` and `author_routes` are accepted on `verifier` too but have no\n286\teffect there (only finder selection reads them).\n287\t\n288\tKeys of `author_routes` can be either an exact author model ID\n289\t(lower-cased) or a family name; see 2.10.\n290\t\n291\t#### `review`\n292\t\n293\t| Key | Type | Default | Validation |\n294\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n295\t| `confidence_floor` | number | `0.6` | `0..1`; `0` -> default |\n296\t| `quick_confidence_floor` | number | `0.8` | `0..1`; `0` -> default |\n297\t| `excludes` | array of string | `[]` | none |\n298\t| `snapshot` | boolean | `true` | none |\n299\t\n300\tAn explicit `0` floor cannot be expressed: it becomes the default. Both\n301\tfloors are validated to `0..1` (deliberate change 20); a value outside the\n302\trange is an error `<key> must be between 0 and 1 (got <value>)`, checked\n303\tin `normalize` before the floor defaults apply. `excludes` are added\n304\tbefore any `--exclude` flags.\n305\t\n306\t#### `prompts`\n307\t\n308\t| Key | Type | Meaning |\n309\t| --- | --- | --- |\n310\t| `finder_code` | string | Finder prompt for code changes |\n311\t| `finder_prose` | string | Finder prompt for prose changes |\n312\t| `verifier` | string | Verifier prompt |\n313\t\n314\t`\"\"` is unset (the embedded template is used). The raw strings are part\n315\tof the trust projection (trust area). Callers read the file through\n316\t`promptPath` (2.8), never the raw string. Contents are used verbatim.\n317\t\n318\t#### `limits`\n319\t\n320\tEvery key is optional; unset means the CLI default (2.1). Optional numbers\n321\tdistinguish an explicit `0` from unset.\n322\t\n323\t| Key | Type | Minimum |\n324\t| --- | --- | --- |\n325\t| `deadline` | duration string | `>= 0` |\n326\t| `request_timeout` | duration string | `>= 0` |\n327\t| `max_total_tokens` | integer | `0` |\n328\t| `max_diff_bytes` | integer | `1` |\n329\t| `max_prompt_bytes` | integer | `1` |\n330\t| `max_file_ctx_bytes` | integer | `1` |\n331\t| `max_attempts` | integer | `1` |\n332\t| `max_verified` | integer | `0` |\n333\t\n334\t#### `interactive`\n335\t\n336\t| Key | Type | Minimum |\n337\t| --- | --- | --- |\n338\t| `min_files` | integer | `0` |\n339\t| `min_diff_lines` | integer | `0` |\n340\t\n341\t#### `gate`\n342\t\n343\t| Key | Type | Validation |\n344\t| --- | --- | --- |\n345\t| `timeout_seconds` | integer | `>= 0` |\n346\t| `severity` | string | `INFO`, `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `CRITICAL`, any case |\n347\t| `offer` | boolean | none (`\"no\"` is a type error) |\n348\t\n349\t`severity` is stored as written; consumers upper-case it. `\"\"` is unset.\n350\t\n351\t#### Settings validation order and messages\n352\t\n353\tAfter profiles and roles, defaults for the two confidence floors are\n354\tapplied, then settings are checked in this fixed order (first failure\n355\twins):\n356\t\n357\t1. Integers below their minimum, in the order `limits.max_total_tokens`,\n358\t   `limits.max_diff_bytes`, `limits.max_prompt_bytes`,\n359\t   `limits.max_file_ctx_bytes`, `limits.max_attempts`,\n360\t   `limits.max_verified`, `interactive.min_files`,\n361\t   `interactive.min_diff_lines`, `gate.timeout_seconds`:\n362\t   `<key> must be an integer >= <min> (got <value>)`.\n363\t2. Negative durations, `limits.deadline` then `limits.request_timeout`:\n364\t   `<key> cannot be negative`.\n365\t3. `gate.severity`: `gate.severity must be INFO, LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, or\n366\t   CRITICAL (got \"<value>\")`.\n367\t\n368\tInteger fields reject non-integers (`1.5`), non-integer lexemes (`1.0`,\n369\t`1e3`), and non-numbers as type errors during the structural decode.\n370\t\n371\t#### Duration strings\n372\t\n373\tA duration is a JSON string in Go `time.ParseDuration` syntax, parsed by\n374\tthe shared helper `parseGoDuration` (this module owns it; the CLI area\n375\tuses it for flags):\n376\t\n377\t- Grammar: optional sign `+` or `-`, then one or more `<number><unit>`\n378\t  groups, where `<number>` is digits with an optional fraction (`1`,\n379\t  `1.5`, `.5`, `1.`; at least one digit overall) and `<unit>` is `ns`,\n380\t  `us`, `µs` (U+00B5), `μs` (U+03BC), `ms`, `s`, `m`, `h`. The bare string\n381\t  `0` (optionally signed) is also valid. A missing unit (`\"10\"`) is\n382\t  invalid. Results that overflow a signed 64-bit nanosecond count are\n383\t  invalid.\n384\t- A non-string value: error `duration must be a string such as \"90s\" (got\n385\t  <raw JSON>)`.\n386\t- An unparseable string: error `invalid duration \"<value>\"`.\n387\t- A negative result is rejected by settings validation (`-1s`), but\n388\t  `-0s` is zero and accepted.\n389\t- Represent the value as integer milliseconds or a `bigint` of\n390\t  nanoseconds; Go keeps nanoseconds. Sub-millisecond precision only\n391\t  matters for equality with Go in tests.\n392\t\n393\t### 2.5 Role resolution\n394\t\n395\t`resolveRole(cfg, name, ctx)` expands a role into a `ResolvedRole`:\n396\t\n397\t1. If `roles[name]` is absent or its profile is empty: error `role \"<name>\"\n398\t   is not configured`.\n399\t2. `resolveProfile(name, profileName, selection)`:\n400\t   - Unknown profile: `role <name> references unknown inference profile\n401\t     \"<profile>\"` (unreachable after load validation except via author\n402\t     routes).\n403\t   - Fields: `profile` (name), `baseURL` = effective endpoint,\n404\t     `explicitEndpoint` = profile set `endpoint`, `model`, `protocol`,\n405\t     `maxOutputTokens`, `sessionHeader`, `pin` and `authorRoutes` from the\n406\t     selection, and the attached stored-credential store.\n407\t   - Credential from `resolveCredential` (2.6). An error is wrapped as\n408\t     `role <name>: <cause>`.\n409\t\n410\tOnly the credential comes from outside the config. In particular these\n411\tenvironment variables have NO effect: `ROAST_<ROLE>_BASE_URL`,\n412\t`ROAST_<ROLE>_PROTOCOL`, `ROAST_<ROLE>_MODEL`,\n413\t`ROAST_<ROLE>_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS`, `OPENAI_BASE_URL`, `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL`,\n414\tand any author-model variable.\n415\t\n416\t`ResolvedRole.apiKey` is secret: it must never be copied into run\n417\tmetadata, logs, debug output, or error messages.\n418\t\n419\t### 2.6 Credential resolution\n420\t\n421\t`<ROLE>` below is `name.toUpperCase()`; the role variable is\n422\t`ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`. An environment variable counts only when its value\n423\tis non-empty.\n424\t\n425\t`resolveCredential(roleName, profile, role)`:\n426\t\n427\t1. Role variable `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY` set -> use it,\n428\t   source `{kind: 'role_env', env: 'ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY'}`.\n429\t2. Profile `api_key_env` named:\n430\t   1. That variable set -> use it, source `{kind: 'profile_env', env}`.\n431\t   2. Else, `ambientCredential(role.protocol, role.baseURL, stored)`.\n432\t      If it returns a key, use it with the ambient source. If it errors,\n433\t      return the error. If it returns no key, return no key with source\n434\t      `{kind: 'profile_env', env: <api_key_env>}` (no `withheld`).\n435\t3. Otherwise return `ambientCredential(role.protocol, role.baseURL,\n436\t   stored)`.\n437\t\n438\t`ambientCredential(protocol, endpoint, stored)`:\n439\t\n440\t1. `provider = providerAt(endpoint, protocol)` (2.7). If found:\n441\t   1. `env[provider.keyEnv]` set -> use it, source\n442\t      `{kind: 'provider_env', env: provider.keyEnv}`.\n443\t   2. `storedCredential(stored, protocol, endpoint)`: if it errors,\n444\t      return the error; if it returns a key, use it.\n445\t2. Return no key with source `{kind: 'none', withheld:\n446\t   withheldKey(protocol)}`.\n447\t\n448\t`storedCredential(stored, protocol, endpoint)`: if `providerAt` finds no\n449\tprovider, or no store is attached, return none. Else\n450\t`stored.lookup(provider.name)`; an error is returned as is; an empty key\n451\tis none; else source `{kind: 'stored', provider: provider.name, scope}`.\n452\tThe registry alone decides the provider, so a custom endpoint never\n453\treceives a stored key.\n454\t\n455\t`withheldKey(protocol)`: candidates, in order, are the standard key\n456\tvariable of the provider at `defaultBaseURL(protocol)` (`OPENAI_API_KEY`\n457\tfor `responses`/`chat` and any unknown protocol, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for\n458\t`anthropic`), then every registry provider's `keyEnv` in registry order\n459\t(`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`). Return the\n460\tfirst one that is set, else `\"\"`. Because it is called only after no key\n461\twas sent, any variable it names was withheld.\n462\t\n463\tResulting precedence (for the default store):\n464\t\n465\t1. `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`\n466\t2. the profile's `api_key_env`\n467\t3. the built-in provider's standard variable (only on that provider's\n468\t   endpoint with a protocol it accepts there)\n469\t4. the provider's project-scope stored credential (same condition)\n470\t5. the provider's user-scope stored credential (same condition)\n471\t\n472\tSteps 4 and 5 are inside the store (`lookup` returns project before\n473\tuser); see the trust-and-credentials area.\n474\t\n475\tStored-credential interface (implemented by the credentials module, in\n476\tthe trust-and-credentials area):\n477\t\n478\t```ts\n479\texport interface StoredCredentials {\n480\t    // '' key when none is stored. Rejects (throws) when a credentials\n481\t    // file cannot be used; it never skips a bad file.\n482\t    lookup(provider:string):{key:string, scope:'project'|'user'|''}\n483\t}\n484\t```\n485\t\n486\tThe store is read lazily: a broken credentials file fails resolution only\n487\twhen a role actually reaches step 4 (no environment credential first).\n488\tThe CLI attaches `credentials.newStore('.')` to every config it loads\n489\t(`withStoredCredentials`); any new code path that resolves roles from a\n490\tloaded config must attach it too.\n491\t\n492\t`CredentialSource`:\n493\t\n494\t```ts\n495\ttype CredentialKind =\n496\t    'role_env'|'profile_env'|'provider_env'|'stored'|'none'\n497\tinterface CredentialSource {\n498\t    kind:CredentialKind\n499\t    env?:string       // variable read, for env kinds\n500\t    provider?:string  // stored: provider name\n501\t    scope?:string     // stored: 'project' or 'user'\n502\t    withheld?:string  // none: a set provider key that was not sent\n503\t}\n504\t```\n505\t\n506\tIts human-readable form (`describeSource`), never containing a secret:\n507\t\n508\t| Kind | Text |\n509\t| --- | --- |\n510\t| `role_env` | `<env> (role override)` |\n511\t| `profile_env` | `<env> (profile api_key_env)` |\n512\t| `provider_env` | `<env> (ambient provider key)` |\n513\t| `stored` | `stored <scope> credential for <provider>` |\n514\t| `none`, withheld | see below |\n515\t| `none` | `none` |\n516\t\n517\tThe withheld form is `none; <withheld> is set but is sent only to its\n518\tprovider's default endpoint`.\n519\t\n520\t`credentialEnvNames(cfg)`: every non-empty `api_key_env` across all\n521\tprofiles (duplicates allowed), so run-record capture can always redact\n522\tthem (record area).\n523\t\n524\t### 2.7 Built-in providers\n525\t\n526\tRegistry, in this order (order matters for `withheldKey` and listings):\n527\t\n528\t1. `openai`\n529\t   - endpoint `https://api.openai.com/v1`\n530\t   - protocols `responses`, `chat` (first = the one starter profiles use)\n531\t   - keyEnv `OPENAI_API_KEY`\n532\t   - keyPage `https://platform.openai.com/api-keys`\n533\t   - login `paste`\n534\t2. `anthropic`\n535\t   - endpoint `https://api.anthropic.com/v1`\n536\t   - protocols `anthropic`\n537\t   - keyEnv `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`\n538\t   - keyPage `https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys`\n539\t   - login `paste`\n540\t3. `openrouter`\n541\t   - endpoint `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1`\n542\t   - protocols `chat`, `responses`\n543\t   - keyEnv `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`\n544\t   - keyPage `https://openrouter.ai/settings/keys`\n545\t   - login `browser`\n546\t\n547\tLogin methods: `paste` opens the key page and reads a pasted key;\n548\t`browser` runs a browser key exchange (OpenRouter PKCE, login area).\n549\t\n550\tInvariant: no two entries share an (endpoint, protocol) pair, so\n551\t`providerAt` finds at most one.\n552\t\n553\t- `builtinProviders()`: copies of the registry (callers cannot mutate\n554\t  it).\n555\t- `lookupProvider(name)`: the provider with exactly this name\n556\t  (case-sensitive), or undefined.\n557\t- `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)`: the first provider that accepts\n558\t  `protocol` and whose endpoint `sameEndpoint`s `endpoint`, or undefined.\n559\t- `defaultBaseURL(protocol)`: `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` for\n560\t  `anthropic`, otherwise `https://api.openai.com/v1` (including unknown\n561\t  protocols). OpenRouter is never a protocol's implicit default. A\n562\t  profile that omits `endpoint` uses this.\n563\t\n564\t`sameEndpoint(a, b)`: trim both and parse as URLs; if either fails to\n565\tparse or has an empty host, false. Otherwise equal when scheme matches\n566\tcase-insensitively, host (including port) matches case-insensitively,\n567\tpaths match after stripping trailing `/` characters, raw query strings\n568\tmatch exactly, and userinfo matches exactly.\n569\t\n570\tExamples (`providerAt`): `https://api.openai.com/v1/` + `chat` ->\n571\topenai; `HTTPS://API.OPENAI.COM/v1` + `responses` -> openai;\n572\t`https://api.openai.com/v1` + `anthropic` -> none;\n573\t`https://api.anthropic.com/v1` + `chat` -> none; OpenRouter + `responses`\n574\t-> openrouter; OpenRouter + `anthropic` -> none; `http://api.openai.com/v1`\n575\t-> none; `https://api.openai.com/v2` -> none; `\"\"` -> none.\n576\t\n577\tConsumers: trust standardness (trust area: a profile on a built-in\n578\tprovider's endpoint, with an accepted protocol and the standard key\n579\tvariable, needs no approval), ambient credentials (2.6), the `init`\n580\tstarter (doctor/init area), `login`/`logout` provider lookup and\n581\t`providersNeedingLogin` (login area), and the credentials file's provider\n582\tvalidation. Adding a provider means adding a registry row only.\n583\t\n584\t### 2.8 Prompt paths\n585\t\n586\t`promptPath(cfg, p)`: `\"\"` stays `\"\"`; an absolute path is returned as\n587\tis; if the config has no recorded `dir` (not loaded from a file) the path\n588\tis returned as is; otherwise `path.join(cfg.dir, p)` (normalized). So the\n589\tsame file is read wherever in the repository Roast runs. The trust\n590\tprojection hashes the raw string, so approvals survive worktree moves.\n591\t\n592\t### 2.9 Endpoint overrides (`--<role>-base-url`, `--<role>-protocol`)\n593\t\n594\t`overrideEndpoint(roleName, role, baseURL?, protocol?)` applies the\n595\toverrides, then re-binds the credential. `undefined` means the flag was\n596\tnot given (an explicitly given empty string is still an override). It\n597\tfails only when a stored credential the role now needs cannot be read.\n598\tThe CLI calls it only when at least one of the two flags was given, then\n599\tapplies `--<role>-model` and `--<role>-max-output-tokens` directly.\n600\t\n601\t`applyEndpointOverrides(role, baseURL?, protocol?)` returns\n602\t`crossedProvider`:\n603\t\n604\t1. `crossed = false`.\n605\t2. If `protocol` given: `previous = defaultBaseURL(role.protocol)`;\n606\t   `role.protocol = protocol`; `next = defaultBaseURL(protocol)`. If\n607\t   `next !== previous`:\n608\t   - if `!role.explicitEndpoint`: `role.baseURL = next`, `crossed = true`;\n609\t   - else if `sameEndpoint(role.baseURL, previous)`: `role.baseURL =\n610\t     next`, `crossed = true` (`explicitEndpoint` stays true);\n611\t   - else the explicit custom endpoint stays.\n612\t3. If `baseURL` given: `role.baseURL = baseURL`, `explicitEndpoint =\n613\t   true`, `crossed = false`.\n614\t\n615\t`rebindCredential(roleName, role, crossed)`:\n616\t\n617\t1. `role_env`: keep the credential.\n618\t2. `profile_env` and not `crossed`: keep it (even when its key is empty).\n619\t   A profile credential follows an explicit endpoint override.\n620\t3. Otherwise: if `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY` is set, use it (`role_env`).\n621\t   Else `ambientCredential(role.protocol, role.baseURL, stored)`; an\n622\t   error becomes `role <name>: <cause>`.\n623\t\n624\tConsequences (pinned by tests):\n625\t\n626\tEach item reads \"start; override -> endpoint, key\". \"Implicit\" means\n627\tthe profile sets no `endpoint`; \"profile key\" is the `api_key_env` value.\n628\t\n629\t1. implicit, `api_key_env` set; protocol `anthropic` -> Anthropic\n630\t   default, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`.\n631\t2. explicit `https://api.openai.com/v1/`, `api_key_env`; protocol\n632\t   `anthropic` -> Anthropic default, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`.\n633\t3. explicit custom endpoint, `api_key_env`; protocol `anthropic` ->\n634\t   unchanged endpoint, profile key.\n635\t4. implicit, `api_key_env`; protocol `chat` -> OpenAI default, profile\n636\t   key.\n637\t5. implicit, `api_key_env`; protocol `anthropic` and a base URL -> the\n638\t   chosen URL, profile key.\n639\t6. OpenRouter chat with `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`; base URL -> chosen URL, no\n640\t   key.\n641\t7. OpenRouter chat; protocol `responses` -> OpenRouter,\n642\t   `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`.\n643\t8. OpenRouter chat; protocol `anthropic` -> OpenRouter, no key.\n644\t9. implicit with `OPENAI_API_KEY`; base URL -> chosen URL, no key.\n645\t10. `anthropic` protocol, only an anthropic stored key; custom base URL\n646\t    -> no key.\n647\t11. implicit `anthropic`, only an anthropic stored key; protocol\n648\t    `responses` -> OpenAI default, no key.\n649\t12. explicit Anthropic default, only an anthropic stored key; protocol\n650\t    `chat` -> OpenAI default, no key.\n651\t13. implicit `anthropic`, stored keys for every provider; protocol\n652\t    `responses` -> the project-scope OpenAI stored key.\n653\t14. OpenRouter chat, unset `api_key_env` value, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` set;\n654\t    protocol `responses` -> same key before and after.\n655\t\n656\tInitial resolution (`resolveCredential`) and re-binding must agree: both\n657\treach the provider variable and the stored key only through\n658\t`ambientCredential`, so a role picks the same credential with or without\n659\ta same-provider protocol override.\n660\t\n661\t### 2.10 Model families and finder flip\n662\t\n663\t`inferFamily(model)`: provider-neutral family, or `\"\"`.\n664\t\n665\t1. `m = model.trim().toLowerCase()`; empty -> `\"\"`.\n666\t2. If `m` contains `/`: split at the first `/`. If the prefix is one of\n667\t   `anthropic`, `openai`, `google`, `gemini`, `mistral`, `xai`, `cohere`,\n668\t   `deepseek`, return `canonicalFamily(prefix)`. Otherwise `m` = the part\n669\t   after the first `/`.\n670\t3. Strip a trailing `:api`, then a trailing `[1m]` (in that order, each\n671\t   at most once).\n672\t4. Match, first rule wins:\n673\t   - `anthropic`, or prefix `claude-` -> `anthropic`\n674\t   - `openai`, or prefix `gpt-`, `codex-`, `chatgpt-`, `o1`, `o3`, `o4`\n675\t     -> `openai`\n676\t   - `google`, `gemini`, or prefix `gemini-` -> `google`\n677\t   - `mistral`, or prefix `mistral-`, `codestral-` -> `mistral`\n678\t   - `xai`, or prefix `grok-` -> `xai`\n679\t   - `cohere`, or prefix `command-` -> `cohere`\n680\t   - `deepseek`, or prefix `deepseek-` -> `deepseek`\n681\t   - else `\"\"`.\n682\t\n683\t`canonicalFamily(f)`: `gemini` -> `google`, else `f`.\n684\t\n685\tExamples: `anthropic/claude-sonnet` -> anthropic; `gpt-5-codex` -> openai;\n686\t`codex-mini-latest` -> openai; `openai/gpt-6.1-terra` -> openai;\n687\t`google/gemini-2.5-pro` -> google; `grok-4` -> xai;\n688\t`meta-llama/llama-4:free` -> `\"\"`.\n689\t\n690\t`resolveFinderFlip(cfg, finder, authorModel, ctx)` returns\n691\t`{role, note?, warn?}` (the CLI prints them; wording in the CLI area may be\n692\tequivalent):\n693\t\n694\t1. `authorKey = authorModel.trim().toLowerCase()`; empty -> return the\n695\t   finder unchanged.\n696\t2. `authorFamily = inferFamily(authorModel)`.\n697\t3. If `finder.pin`: keep the finder; if `authorFamily !== ''` and\n698\t   `inferFamily(finder.model) === authorFamily`, warn \"author-model <a>\n699\t   shares the finder family <f>, but the finder is pinned; generation\n700\t   independence is reduced\".\n701\t4. `target = finder.authorRoutes[authorKey]`, else (when `authorFamily`\n702\t   is non-empty) `finder.authorRoutes[authorFamily]`.\n703\t5. No target: keep the finder. If `authorFamily === ''` warn \"could not\n704\t   determine a model family for author-model \"<a>\" and no exact author\n705\t   route is configured; keeping finder <model>\"; else if the finder's\n706\t   family equals `authorFamily` warn \"author-model <a> shares the finder\n707\t   family <f> and no author route is configured; generation independence\n708\t   is reduced\".\n709\t6. Target found: `resolved = resolveProfile('finder', target, {})` (full\n710\t   credential resolution under the `finder` role variable; `pin` false,\n711\t   no routes). An error is returned.\n712\t7. `targetFamily = inferFamily(resolved.model)`. If (`authorFamily` is\n713\t   non-empty and (`targetFamily` is empty or equals `authorFamily`)) or\n714\t   `resolved.model` equals `authorModel` case-insensitively: keep the\n715\t   original finder, warn \"author-model <a> shares the finder family <f>\n716\t   and profile \"<target>\" uses non-independent model \"<m>\"; keeping\n717\t   finder <model>\".\n718\t8. Else use `resolved`, with note \"author-model <a> matched an exact\n719\t   route; using finder profile <target> (<model>)\" when `authorFamily` is\n720\t   empty, or \"author-model <a> maps to family <f>; using finder profile\n721\t   <target> (<model>)\".\n722\t\n723\tAn author-route profile follows the same credential rules (2.6): a route\n724\tto a custom endpoint without `api_key_env` gets no ambient key.\n725\t\n726\t### 2.11 Role validation before any model call\n727\t\n728\t`isPlaceholderModel(model)`: after trimming, length > 2, starts with `<`\n729\tand ends with `>` (the starter uses `<finder-model>` and\n730\t`<verifier-model>`; defined with the starter in the doctor/init area, used\n731\there).\n732\t\n733\t`validateRole(name, role)`, first failure wins:\n734\t\n735\t1. `role.model.trim() === ''`: `<name> model is not configured (set\n736\t   inference_profiles.<profile>.model or --<name>-model)`.\n737\t2. Placeholder model: `<name> model <model> is a placeholder: set\n738\t   inference_profiles.<profile>.model in the project config to a model\n739\t   ID, or pass --<name>-model`.\n740\t3. `role.apiKey.trim() === ''`, with `provider = providerAt(role.baseURL,\n741\t   role.protocol)`:\n742\t   - built-in and source `profile_env`: `<name> API key is not configured\n743\t     (run `npx roast login <provider>`, or set <env>, named by\n744\t     inference_profiles.<profile>.api_key_env, or <roleVar>)`\n745\t   - built-in: `<name> API key is not configured (run `npx roast login\n746\t     <provider>`, or set <keyEnv> or <roleVar>)`\n747\t   - source `profile_env`: `<name> API key is not configured (set <env>,\n748\t     named by inference_profiles.<profile>.api_key_env, or <roleVar>)`\n749\t   - `withheld` set: `<name> API key is not configured: <withheld> is not\n750\t     sent to <baseURL> because it is not that provider's default endpoint\n751\t     (set inference_profiles.<profile>.api_key_env or <roleVar>)`\n752\t   - else: `<name> API key is not configured (set\n753\t     inference_profiles.<profile>.api_key_env or <roleVar>)`\n754\t4. `validateEndpoint(name, role.baseURL, role.protocol,\n755\t   role.maxOutputTokens)` (2.4 endpoint rule, with field = role name).\n756\t   This is where a flag-supplied protocol, endpoint, or output limit is\n757\t   validated.\n758\t\n759\tThe CLI turns these into usage errors before any request (CLI area);\n760\t`doctor` reports them per role (doctor area).\n761\t\n762\t### 2.12 Unported Go helpers\n763\t\n764\t`DescribeCredential`, `IsLoopbackHost` (localhost or a loopback IP,\n765\ttrailing `.` ignored), and `InferenceProfile.DescribeEndpoint` have no\n766\tnon-test callers at the baseline. They are not ported (deliberate change\n767\t23 in `spec.md`); `doctor` uses `describeSource` instead.\n768\t\n769\t## 3. TypeScript module design\n770\t\n771\tAll modules live under `src/config/`; tests under `test/config/`. Config\n772\tdata keeps the JSON key spelling (snake_case) so the zod schema's inferred\n773\ttype is the config type; runtime role objects use camelCase.\n774\t\n775\t### Parsing pipeline\n776\t\n777\t`decode(text)` is staged so the Go error order (2.3) holds:\n778\t\n779\t1. `parseJsonc(text)` (`src/config/jsonc.ts`): `jsonc-parser`'s\n780\t   `parseTree` with `allowTrailingComma: true` (the `init` starter writes\n781\t   a trailing comma) and comments allowed. Any parse error, including\n782\t   `EndOfFileExpected` for trailing content, is fatal. Convert the tree\n783\t   into plain values whose objects have a null prototype, assigning keys\n784\t   with `Object.defineProperty` so a key named `__proto__` (a legal\n785\t   profile name in Go) is an ordinary key; a duplicate key keeps the last\n786\t   value (same as Go). Keys are kept as written and matched exactly, so\n787\t   `\"Version\"` is an unknown field. A `visit` pass (jsonc-parser's\n788\t   visitor) records the raw token text of every number; a number whose\n789\t   lexeme is not a plain integer (`1.0`, `1e3`) is flagged by path, and\n790\t   the schema step reports a type error if that path is an integer\n791\t   field.\n792\t2. `checkVersion(value)`: not a plain object -> type error; `version`\n793\t   absent or `null` -> required; not an integer -> type error; not 1 ->\n794\t   unsupported.\n795\t3. `rejectLiteralCredentials(value)`.\n796\t4. `nullsAsUnset(value)`: Go parity for `null` (2.3). In fixed-shape\n797\t   objects a `null` property is deleted. In the map-shaped objects a\n798\t   `null` value becomes the zero value: an `inference_profiles` or `roles`\n799\t   entry becomes `{}`, an `author_routes` entry becomes `\"\"`. A `null`\n800\t   element of `review.excludes` becomes `\"\"`.\n801\t5. `rejectLoneSurrogates(value)`: any key or string value containing a\n802\t   lone surrogate is an error, so the trust hash is always defined.\n803\t   Then `ConfigSchema.safeParse(value)`: strict structure and types. Zod\n804\t   issues map to messages `<dotted.path>: <reason>`, with unknown keys\n805\t   reported as `unknown field \"<key>\"` at their path. Wording need only be\n806\t   equivalent to Go's.\n807\t6. `normalize(raw, dir)`: profile defaults and checks (2.4), role checks,\n808\t   confidence-floor defaults, then `validateSettings`. These run as plain\n809\t   functions rather than zod refinements so their order and messages are\n810\t   explicit and so the JSON Schema generator never sees them.\n811\t\n812\t### `src/config/schema.ts`\n813\t\n814\tThe zod (v4) schema is the single source of truth for shape, types, and\n815\tthe editor schema. Every field carries `.meta({description})` with the\n816\tdescription text currently in `roast.schema.json` (defaults included).\n817\t\n818\t```ts\n819\timport {z} from 'zod'\n820\t\n821\texport const Duration = z.string()\n822\t    .refine(isGoDuration, 'invalid duration')\n823\t    .meta({\n824\t        pattern: '^([0-9]+(\\\\.[0-9]+)?(ns|us|µs|ms|s|m|h))+$|^0$',\n825\t        description: 'A Go duration such as \"90s\" or \"10m\".'\n826\t    })\n827\t\n828\texport const InferenceProfile = z.strictObject({\n829\t    endpoint: z.string().optional(),       // format: uri in meta\n830\t    api_key_env: z.string()\n831\t        .regex(/^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$/).optional(),\n832\t    model: z.string().optional(),\n833\t    protocol: z.enum(['responses', 'chat', 'anthropic']).optional(),\n834\t    max_output_tokens: z.int().optional(),\n835\t    session_header: z.string().optional(),\n836\t})\n837\t// ...Role, Review, Prompts, Limits, Interactive, Gate likewise\n838\texport const ConfigSchema = z.strictObject({\n839\t    $schema: z.string().optional(),\n840\t    version: z.literal(1),\n841\t    inference_profiles: z.record(z.string(), InferenceProfile).optional(),\n842\t    roles: z.strictObject({\n843\t        finder: Role.optional(),\n844\t        verifier: Role.optional(),\n845\t    }).optional(),\n846\t    // review, prompts, limits, interactive, gate\n847\t})\n848\texport type RawConfig = z.infer<typeof ConfigSchema>\n849\t```\n850\t\n851\tSchema-vs-runtime rules:\n852\t\n853\t- Where Go accepts an empty string as \"unset\" but the editor schema shows\n854\t  a constraint (`api_key_env` pattern, `protocol` enum), the zod check\n855\t  must accept `\"\"` too (for example `.or(z.literal(''))`) while the\n856\t  generated JSON Schema keeps the constraint. Use zod 4's\n857\t  `toJSONSchema` `override` hook, or metadata, for every place where the\n858\t  editor schema is intentionally stricter than or different from the\n859\t  runtime check. Each such place is listed in a comment next to the\n860\t  field. They are: `protocol` and `api_key_env` (`\"\"` accepted),\n861\t  `gate.severity` (runtime accepts any letter case, schema shows the\n862\t  upper-case enum), `review.confidence_floor` and\n863\t  `quick_confidence_floor` (schema shows `minimum: 0`, `maximum: 1`;\n864\t  runtime checks the same range in `normalize`), `max_output_tokens`\n865\t  (schema `minimum: 1`,\n866\t  runtime accepts `0` as default and rejects negatives in `normalize`),\n867\t  `session_header` (schema `maxLength: 64` only), and the integer\n868\t  minimums (schema `minimum`, runtime checks in `validateSettings` so\n869\t  the messages and order match 2.4).\n870\t- Roles under `roles` other than `finder`/`verifier` produce the specific\n871\t  message `roles.<name> is not a role (finder or verifier)`, not a\n872\t  generic unknown-key message.\n873\t\n874\t### Schema generation\n875\t\n876\t`scripts/gen-schema.ts` (bundled with esbuild like the tests, then run by\n877\tNode) builds the editor schema and writes `roast.schema.json` (2-space\n878\tindent, trailing newline):\n879\t\n880\t```ts\n881\tconst schema = {\n882\t    $schema: 'http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#',\n883\t    $id: 'https://github.com/nichoth/roast/roast.schema.json',\n884\t    title: 'Roast project config (.roast.json)',\n885\t    description: 'Every non-secret Roast setting. ...',\n886\t    ...z.toJSONSchema(ConfigSchema, {\n887\t        target: 'draft-7', io: 'input', override: editorOverrides\n888\t    })\n889\t}\n890\t```\n891\t\n892\tThe module exports `renderProjectSchema():string` (in\n893\t`src/config/schema.ts`), which returns the full file text; the packaging\n894\tarea's build calls it to generate `roast.schema.json`, and\n895\t`scripts/gen-schema.ts` is a thin wrapper around it. npm script\n896\t`build:schema` runs it. The test `test/config/schema.ts`\n897\tgenerates the schema in memory and asserts it deep-equals the committed\n898\tfile (parsed JSON, not text), failing with \"run npm run build:schema\".\n899\tThis replaces Go's reflection-based drift test. The file stays in\n900\t`package.json` `files`, and the starter's `$schema` value stays\n901\t`./node_modules/@substrate-system/roast/roast.schema.json`.\n902\t\n903\t### `src/config/load.ts`\n904\t\n905\t```ts\n906\texport const PROJECT_CONFIG_NAME = '.roast.json'\n907\texport const CURRENT_VERSION = 1\n908\t\n909\texport interface ProjectConfig extends NormalizedConfig {\n910\t    readonly dir:string            // '' when not loaded from a file\n911\t    readonly stored?:StoredCredentials\n912\t}\n913\texport function findConfig(start:string, stop:string):string\n914\texport function loadConfig(path:string):ProjectConfig\n915\texport function decode(text:string):RawConfig\n916\texport function normalize(raw:RawConfig, dir:string):ProjectConfig\n917\texport function withStoredCredentials(\n918\t    cfg:ProjectConfig,\n919\t    store:StoredCredentials\n920\t):ProjectConfig\n921\texport function promptPath(cfg:ProjectConfig, p:string):string\n922\texport function credentialEnvNames(cfg:ProjectConfig):string[]\n923\texport function snapshotEnabled(cfg:ProjectConfig):boolean\n924\texport function gateOffers(cfg:ProjectConfig):boolean\n925\t```\n926\t\n927\t`NormalizedConfig` is `RawConfig` with `inference_profiles` and `roles`\n928\talways present (possibly empty), every profile's `protocol` and\n929\t`max_output_tokens` filled, `author_routes` keys normalized, and both\n930\tconfidence floors filled. Optional numbers stay `number|undefined` so an\n931\texplicit `0` differs from unset; `limits.deadline` and\n932\t`limits.request_timeout` are stored parsed (`GoDuration`, below).\n933\t\n934\tErrors are thrown as `ConfigError` (a plain `Error` subclass); the CLI\n935\twraps them as usage errors. Filesystem calls are synchronous (`node:fs`),\n936\twhich keeps `loadConfig` and `resolveRole` synchronous.\n937\t\n938\t### `src/config/duration.ts`\n939\t\n940\t```ts\n941\texport type GoDuration = bigint            // nanoseconds\n942\texport function parseGoDuration(s:string):GoDuration  // throws\n943\texport function isGoDuration(s:string):boolean\n944\texport function toMillis(d:GoDuration):number\n945\t```\n946\t\n947\tShared with the CLI area for `--deadline` and `--request-timeout`.\n948\t\n949\t### `src/config/provider.ts`\n950\t\n951\t```ts\n952\texport type Protocol = 'chat'|'responses'|'anthropic'\n953\texport type LoginMethod = 'paste'|'browser'\n954\texport interface BuiltinProvider {\n955\t    readonly name:string\n956\t    readonly endpoint:string\n957\t    readonly protocols:readonly Protocol[]\n958\t    readonly keyEnv:string\n959\t    readonly keyPage:string\n960\t    readonly login:LoginMethod\n961\t}\n962\texport function builtinProviders():BuiltinProvider[]\n963\texport function lookupProvider(name:string):BuiltinProvider|undefined\n964\texport function providerAt(\n965\t    endpoint:string,\n966\t    protocol:string\n967\t):BuiltinProvider|undefined\n968\texport function defaultBaseURL(protocol:string):string\n969\texport function sameEndpoint(a:string, b:string):boolean\n970\t```\n971\t\n972\tURL parsing uses the WHATWG `URL` class. Only the empty-host case is\n973\tpinned to Go; other odd hosts (spaces, IDNA) follow WHATWG. A value whose text\n974\tafter the scheme does not start with `//` plus a non-empty authority (for\n975\texample `https:///v1` or `https:v1`) is treated as having an empty host\n976\t(WHATWG would otherwise repair it), matching Go. Endpoint strings are\n977\tstored and sent\n978\texactly as written; parsing is only for checks and comparison.\n979\t\n980\t### `src/config/credential.ts`\n981\t\n982\t```ts\n983\texport type Env = Readonly<Record<string, string|undefined>>\n984\texport interface StoredCredentials {\n985\t    lookup(provider:string):{key:string, scope:string}\n986\t}\n987\texport type CredentialKind =\n988\t    'role_env'|'profile_env'|'provider_env'|'stored'|'none'\n989\texport interface CredentialSource { /* 2.6 */ }\n990\texport function describeSource(s:CredentialSource):string\n991\texport function roleKeyVar(role:string):string   // ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY\n992\texport function ambientCredential(\n993\t    protocol:string,\n994\t    endpoint:string,\n995\t    stored:StoredCredentials|undefined,\n996\t    env:Env\n997\t):{key:string, source:CredentialSource}\n998\texport function overrideEndpoint(\n999\t    roleName:string,\n1000\t    role:ResolvedRole,\n1001\t    overrides:{baseURL?:string, protocol?:string},\n1002\t    env:Env\n1003\t):ResolvedRole\n1004\t```\n1005\t\n1006\t`env` is passed explicitly everywhere (never read from `process.env`\n1007\tinside this area) so the in-process CLI seam can inject it.\n1008\t`rebindCredential` and `applyEndpointOverrides` are module-private but\n1009\texported from an `internal` object for the matrix tests.\n1010\t\n1011\t### `src/config/role.ts`\n1012\t\n1013\t```ts\n1014\texport interface ResolvedRole {\n1015\t    readonly profile:string\n1016\t    readonly baseURL:string\n1017\t    readonly apiKey:string            // secret; never logged or recorded\n1018\t    readonly model:string\n1019\t    readonly protocol:string\n1020\t    readonly maxOutputTokens:number\n1021\t    readonly sessionHeader:string\n1022\t    readonly pin:boolean\n1023\t    readonly authorRoutes:Readonly<Record<string, string>>\n1024\t    readonly credential:CredentialSource\n1025\t    readonly explicitEndpoint:boolean\n1026\t    readonly stored?:StoredCredentials\n1027\t}\n1028\texport function resolveRole(\n1029\t    cfg:ProjectConfig,\n1030\t    name:'finder'|'verifier',\n1031\t    env:Env\n1032\t):ResolvedRole\n1033\texport function resolveFinderFlip(\n1034\t    cfg:ProjectConfig,\n1035\t    finder:ResolvedRole,\n1036\t    authorModel:string,\n1037\t    env:Env\n1038\t):{role:ResolvedRole, note?:string, warn?:string}\n1039\texport function validateRole(name:string, role:ResolvedRole):void\n1040\texport function validateEndpoint(\n1041\t    field:string,\n1042\t    baseURL:string,\n1043\t    protocol:string,\n1044\t    maxOutputTokens:number\n1045\t):void\n1046\t```\n1047\t\n1048\t`ResolvedRole` objects are immutable; overrides return a new object.\n1049\t`apiKey` must be excluded from any `toJSON`/debug rendering: give\n1050\t`ResolvedRole` no enumerable path into run metadata, and debug output in\n1051\tthis area logs only the credential source kind and variable name.\n1052\t\n1053\t### `src/config/family.ts`\n1054\t\n1055\t```ts\n1056\texport function inferFamily(model:string):string\n1057\texport function canonicalFamily(family:string):string\n1058\t```\n1059\t\n1060\t### Dependencies\n1061\t\n1062\t- Uses: `jsonc-parser`, `zod`, `node:fs`, `node:path`,\n1063\t  `@substrate-system/debug` (namespace `roast:config`).\n1064\t- Used by: CLI (load, flags, resolution, validation), trust (projection\n1065\t  uses `providerAt`, the effective endpoint, `promptPath` raw strings),\n1066\t  credentials (`lookupProvider`), login/logout (`builtinProviders`,\n1067\t  `lookupProvider`, `providerAt`), doctor (`validateRole`,\n1068\t  `describeSource`), init starter (`inferFamily`, `lookupProvider`,\n1069\t  registry protocol order), record (`credentialEnvNames`), gate\n1070\t  (`findConfig` via `gateConfigPath`, `gate` settings).\n1071\t- The Git toplevel lookup in `projectConfigPath` comes from the scope\n1072\t  area's `gitRoot` helper.\n1073\t- `src/config` must not import the credentials module (the store is\n1074\t  injected through the `StoredCredentials` interface), mirroring Go.\n1075\t\n1076\t## 4. Deliberate changes\n1077\t\n1078\t- Node `>=22.18`, darwin/linux only: no Windows path handling in\n1079\t  `findConfig` or `promptPath`.\n1080\t- Debug tracing under `roast:config` (`DEBUG=roast:*`): may log the\n1081\t  config path found, the profile chosen per role, and the credential\n1082\t  source kind and variable name; never a key, header, or prompt.\n1083\t- Validation messages need only be equivalent to Go's, not identical.\n1084\t- Order of errors among several invalid profiles or roles is document\n1085\t  order (Go's was arbitrary map order).\n1086\t- JSON keys match exactly, case-sensitively (deliberate change 7): Go\n1087\t  accepted `\"Version\"`; here it is an unknown field. `null` still means\n1088\t  absent.\n1089\t- `review.confidence_floor` and `quick_confidence_floor` are validated to\n1090\t  `0..1` (deliberate change 20); Go did not range-check them.\n1091\t- `DescribeCredential`, `IsLoopbackHost`, and `DescribeEndpoint` are not\n1092\t  ported (deliberate change 23).\n1093\t- Strings containing lone surrogates are rejected, so the trust hash is\n1094\t  always defined (\"Bytes and text\" in `spec.md`).\n1095\t- Endpoint URL parsing is WHATWG; only the empty-host case is pinned to\n1096\t  Go.\n1097\t\n1098\tNo other change: every field, default, range, and error condition of\n1099\t`.roast.json` version 1 is kept.\n1100\t\n1101\t## 5. Test plan\n1102\t\n1103\tSeams: config loading and validation are observable through the CLI at\n1104\tlow cost (exit code 2, no request reaches the fake server), so the\n1105\trepresentative cases run at the CLI seam. The credential and override\n1106\tmatrices (about 100 combinations) and pure functions (`inferFamily`,\n1107\t`providerAt`, duration parsing) run at the module seam, because each CLI\n1108\tcase would need a full review against the fake server to observe one\n1109\t`Authorization` header. A handful of credential cases also run at the CLI\n1110\tseam to prove the wiring (which key reaches which endpoint).\n1111\t\n1112\t### Discovery (CLI seam unless noted)\n1113\t\n1114\t- Nearest config wins; nothing above the stop directory is found; a\n1115\t  config at the stop directory is found (`TestFindWalksUpNoFurtherThanStop`,\n1116\t  module seam).\n1117\t- Review in a subdirectory uses the nearest config below the Git\n1118\t  toplevel (`TestReviewUsesNearestProjectConfigBelowGitToplevel`).\n1119\t- `--config` overrides discovery\n1120\t  (`TestConfigFlagOverridesDiscoveredProjectConfig`).\n1121\t- No config: usage error, no request\n1122\t  (`TestReviewWithoutProjectConfigNamesRoastInit`); a user-level config\n1123\t  directory is never read (`TestUserConfigDirectoryIsNotRead`).\n1124\t- Symlinked working directory vs `realpath` toplevel still stops at the\n1125\t  toplevel (new; covers the macOS `/private/var` case).\n1126\t\n1127\t### Parsing and validation\n1128\t\n1129\t- JSONC comments and trailing commas accepted\n1130\t  (`TestLoadAcceptsJSONCCommentsAndTrailingCommas`,\n1131\t  `TestReviewUsesModelsFromJSONCProjectConfig`).\n1132\t- Rejected: unknown top-level field, unknown profile field (`base_url`),\n1133\t  missing/zero/future/string version, literal `api_key`, trailing\n1134\t  document, non-object, YAML (`TestLoadRejectsInvalidProjectConfig`);\n1135\t  literal `api_key` even beside `api_key_env`\n1136\t  (`TestLoadRejectsProfileCredentialAmbiguity`); `upload` section\n1137\t  (`TestLoadRejectsUploadSection`); missing file\n1138\t  (`TestLoadMissingFileIsAnError`); via the CLI as exit 2 with no request\n1139\t  (`TestReviewRejectsInvalidProjectConfig`).\n1140\t- Session header: valid value kept; too long, bad characters, and every\n1141\t  reserved name in any case rejected (`TestLoadSessionHeader`,\n1142\t  `TestLoadRejectsInvalidSessionHeader`).\n1143\t- Limits: `max_diff_bytes: 0`, `max_attempts: 0`, `max_total_tokens: -1`,\n1144\t  `request_timeout: \"later\"`, `deadline: \"-1s\"`, unknown limit rejected\n1145\t  (`TestInvalidLimitsAreRejected`); limits from config bound the review\n1146\t  (`TestLimitsInProjectConfigBoundTheReview`,\n1147\t  `TestMaxDiffBytesInProjectConfigTruncatesTheDiff`,\n1148\t  `TestMaxPromptBytesInProjectConfigRejectsALargePrompt`); flags win for\n1149\t  one invocation (`TestFlagsOverrideProjectConfigForOneInvocation`);\n1150\t  interactive thresholds and `review.snapshot` honored\n1151\t  (`TestInteractiveThresholdsInProjectConfig`,\n1152\t  `TestReviewSnapshotSettingInProjectConfig`).\n1153\t- Gate: `severity: \"SEVERE\"`, `timeout_seconds: -1`, `offer: \"no\"`\n1154\t  rejected (`TestInvalidGateSettingsAreRejected`); settings come from the\n1155\t  config or default (`TestGateSettingsComeFromTheProjectConfig`,\n1156\t  `TestGateSettingsDefaultWhenUnset`); `offer: false` stops the offer\n1157\t  (`TestGateOfferFalseStopsTheGateOffer`).\n1158\t- New parity cases (module seam): `null` fields act as unset; a profile\n1159\t  named `__proto__` loads as an ordinary profile; lower-case\n1160\t  `gate.severity` accepted; author-route keys `\" Gemini \"` and `google`\n1161\t  collide as duplicates; `max_output_tokens: 0` becomes 32768 and `-1`\n1162\t  is rejected.\n1163\t- Tightenings (module seam): `\"Version\": 1` and `\"Model\"` in a profile\n1164\t  are rejected as unknown fields; a duplicate key keeps the last value;\n1165\t  `max_attempts: 1.0` and `1e3` are rejected as non-integer lexemes while\n1166\t  `1` loads; a trailing comma after the last property loads;\n1167\t  `confidence_floor: 1.5` and `-0.1` are rejected, `0`, `0.5`, `1`\n1168\t  accepted; a string containing a lone surrogate (written as an escape\n1169\t  in the JSON) is rejected.\n1170\t- Duration parser: Go grammar accept/reject table, including `0`, `.5s`,\n1171\t  `1h30m`, `µs`/`μs`, `+5s`, `-0s`, and rejects `10`, `\"\"`, `1x`.\n1172\t\n1173\t### Schema\n1174\t\n1175\t- `renderProjectSchema()` output, parsed, deep-equals the committed\n1176\t  `roast.schema.json` (replaces `TestSchemaMatchesConfigStruct`).\n1177\t- Every example config used by tests and the `init` starter validates\n1178\t  against the generated JSON Schema (new; cheap guard that the editor\n1179\t  schema is not stricter than intended for real configs).\n1180\t\n1181\t### Resolution and credentials (module seam, plus CLI wiring)\n1182\t\n1183\t- Named profiles resolve with defaults; Anthropic defaults for the\n1184\t  `anthropic` protocol (`TestLoadResolvesNamedProfiles`,\n1185\t  `TestResolveRoleUsesNativeAnthropicDefaults`).\n1186\t- Legacy environment overrides are ignored\n1187\t  (`TestResolveRoleIgnoresLegacyGlobalOverrides`,\n1188\t  `TestRemovedRoleEnvironmentVariablesAreIgnored`,\n1189\t  `TestRemovedEnvironmentVariablesHaveNoEffect` at the CLI seam).\n1190\t- Precedence matrix: protocol x endpoint (unset, default, custom,\n1191\t  ambient gateway) x `api_key_env` x `*_BASE_URL` set x role key; checks\n1192\t  endpoint, key, source kind, and `withheld`\n1193\t  (`TestCredentialAndEndpointPrecedence`).\n1194\t- `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` only at OpenRouter with chat/responses, withheld\n1195\t  elsewhere (`TestOpenRouterKeyOnlyAtOpenRouter`).\n1196\t- Custom endpoint without a named credential gets no ambient key, and\n1197\t  validation names the withheld variable and the role variable\n1198\t  (`TestTemplateProfileWithoutCredentialNeverReceivesAmbientKey`).\n1199\t- Trailing slash on the default endpoint still matches\n1200\t  (`TestEndpointComparisonIgnoresATrailingSlash`).\n1201\t- Author-route profiles follow the same credential rule\n1202\t  (`TestAuthorRouteProfileUsesTheSameCredentialRule`).\n1203\t- Override table in 2.9 (`TestEndpointFlagOverrides`,\n1204\t  `TestRebindCredential`, `TestStoredCredentialDoesNotFollowAnOverride`,\n1205\t  `TestUnsetProfileVariableOnABuiltinEndpointPrefersProviderVariable`).\n1206\t- Stored credentials: used at a built-in endpoint; ranked below every\n1207\t  env variable, project before user; never sent to a custom endpoint;\n1208\t  an unusable store fails resolution only when reached; the source names\n1209\t  scope and provider, never the key\n1210\t  (`TestStoredCredentialAtABuiltinEndpoint`,\n1211\t  `TestStoredCredentialPrecedence`,\n1212\t  `TestStoredCredentialNeverReachesACustomEndpoint`,\n1213\t  `TestUnusableStoredCredentialsFailResolution`,\n1214\t  `TestStoredCredentialSourceNamesScopeNotSecret`).\n1215\t- No source rendering ever contains a secret\n1216\t  (`TestCredentialSourceNeverIncludesSecret`).\n1217\t- Missing credential on a built-in provider suggests login\n1218\t  (`TestMissingCredentialOnABuiltinProviderSuggestsLogin`); the\n1219\t  `describeCredential` test is not ported.\n1220\t- Embedded userinfo in an endpoint fails validation\n1221\t  (`TestValidateRoleRejectsEmbeddedCredentials`).\n1222\t- CLI wiring: shell provider key reaches only its default endpoint; not a\n1223\t  custom endpoint; a named credential and a role key work on a custom\n1224\t  endpoint (`TestShellProviderKeyGoesOnlyToItsDefaultEndpoint`,\n1225\t  `TestShellProviderKeyIsNotSentToACustomEndpoint`,\n1226\t  `TestNamedCredentialAndRoleKeyOnACustomEndpoint`), asserted on the\n1227\t  `Authorization` header the fake server received.\n1228\t\n1229\t### Providers and families (module seam)\n1230\t\n1231\t- `lookupProvider` returns each built-in by exact name only\n1232\t  (`TestLookupBuiltinProvider`); `providerAt` example table\n1233\t  (`TestProviderAt`); every registered (endpoint, protocol) pair maps\n1234\t  back to its provider (`TestProviderAtIsUnique`).\n1235\t- `inferFamily` table (`TestInferFamily`); finder flip selects a named\n1236\t  profile, an exact route, refuses a same-family target, and treats slash\n1237\t  IDs like bare IDs (`TestResolveFinderFlipSelectsNamedProfile`,\n1238\t  `TestResolveFinderFlipSelectsExactAuthorRoute`,\n1239\t  `TestResolveFinderFlipRejectsSameFamilyTarget`,\n1240\t  `TestResolveFinderFlipTreatsSlashIDsLikeBareIDs`); the CLI flag\n1241\t  selects the flip and the environment does not\n1242\t  (`TestAuthorModelFlagSelectsTheFinderFlip`,\n1243\t  `TestAuthorModelEnvironmentDoesNotFlipTheFinder`). Assert on the\n1244\t  chosen profile and on whether a note or warning exists, not on text.\n1245\t\n1246\t## 6. Go baseline references\n1247\t\n1248\tAt commit `cd08f10`:\n1249\t\n1250\t- `internal/config/config.go` -- types, `Find`, `Load`, decoding,\n1251\t  normalization, settings validation, session header, `ResolveRole`,\n1252\t  credential resolution, overrides, `InferFamily`, finder flip,\n1253\t  `ValidateRole`, `validateEndpoint`.\n1254\t- `internal/config/provider.go` -- built-in provider registry.\n1255\t- `internal/config/starter.go` -- `IsPlaceholderModel`, `SchemaRef`\n1256\t  (starter itself is in the doctor/init area).\n1257\t- `internal/config/trust.go` -- consumer of `ProviderAt` (trust area).\n1258\t- `internal/cli/config.go` -- `projectConfigPath`, `gateConfigPath`,\n1259\t  `loadProjectConfig`, `withStoredCredentials`, usage errors.\n1260\t- `internal/cli/cli.go` -- defaults (lines 156-171), `configInt`,\n1261\t  `flagOrConfigInt`, `flagOrConfigDuration`, `applyRoleFlags`.\n1262\t- `internal/cli/gate.go` -- gate defaults.\n1263\t- `internal/scope/scope.go` -- `GitRoot`.\n1264\t- `roast.schema.json` -- current editor schema and descriptions.\n1265\t- Tests: `internal/config/config_test.go`, `credential_test.go`,\n1266\t  `provider_test.go`, `schema_test.go`, `stored_credential_test.go`;\n1267\t  `internal/cli/project_config_test.go`, `settings_test.go`.\n1268\t\n1269\t## 7. Resolved\n1270\t\n1271\t1. Key case: JSON keys match exactly; `\"Version\"` is an unknown field\n1272\t   (deliberate change 7, `spec.md`). `null` still means absent.\n1273\t2. Integer fields reject non-integer lexemes (`1.0`, `1e3`) by raw token\n1274\t   text through jsonc-parser's visitor (cross-cutting rule \"Config\n1275\t   numbers\", `spec.md`).\n1276\t3. Duplicate keys: last wins, same as Go `encoding/json`.\n1277\t4. `confidence_floor` values are validated to 0..1 (deliberate change 20,\n1278\t   `spec.md`).\n1279\t5. `DescribeCredential`, `IsLoopbackHost`, `DescribeEndpoint` are not\n1280\t   ported (deliberate change 23, `spec.md`).\n1281\t6. WHATWG `URL` is used; only the empty-host case is pinned to Go.\n1282\t7. jsonc-parser runs with `allowTrailingComma: true`; strings with lone\n1283\t   surrogates are rejected (\"Bytes and text\", `spec.md`); the area exports\n1284\t   `renderProjectSchema():string` for the packaging build.\n1285\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"682f1424-ed5e-4ad8-82df-9c53c97a2745","uuid":"9dc101f8-2d1b-4a40-90b4-bf86c114eed0","timestamp":"2026-11-18T20:09:50.710Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-06-providers-and-role-resolution/682f1424-ed5e-4ad8-82df-9c53c97a2745/tool-results/bttdkazba.txt","content":"# Area 02: Project config and built-in providers\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nThis area owns the **project config** (`.roast.json`) and the registry of\n**built-in providers**. It:\n\n1. Finds the project config nearest the working directory, searching\n   upward no further than the Git toplevel.\n2. Parses it as JSONC and validates every field (one zod schema is the\n   source of truth; `roast.schema.json` is generated from it).\n3. Applies defaults and normalizes values (profile protocol, output\n   limit, author-route family names, confidence floors).\n4. Resolves a **role** (finder or verifier) into a runtime-ready role:\n   the **inference profile**'s model, endpoint, protocol, output limit,\n   session header, and the **credential** picked from environment\n   variables or a **stored credential**.\n5. Re-binds the credential when command-line flags move a role to another\n   endpoint or protocol.\n6. Selects an alternate finder profile from the author's model family\n   (author routes) and validates a resolved role before any model call.\n7. Knows each built-in provider's endpoint, accepted protocols, standard\n   key variable, key page, and login method.\n\nOut of scope, referenced only: the trust projection and its hash (area\n\"trust and credentials\"), the stored-credential file format and lookup\norder within the store (same area), the `init` starter config and model\nplacement (area \"doctor and init\"), CLI flag parsing and the use of limits\nduring a review (area \"CLI\").\n\n## 2. Behavior contract\n\n### 2.1 Constants\n\n| Name | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Config file name | `.roast.json` |\n| Supported `version` | `1` |\n| Default `max_output_tokens` | `32768` |\n| Default `review.confidence_floor` | `0.6` |\n| Default `review.quick_confidence_floor` | `0.8` |\n| OpenAI default endpoint | `https://api.openai.com/v1` |\n| Anthropic default endpoint | `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` |\n| OpenRouter endpoint | `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1` |\n| Max `session_header` length | 64 (characters; all are ASCII) |\n| Default profile protocol | `responses` |\n\nThe defaults that the CLI applies to unset `limits`, `interactive`, and\n`gate` settings live in the CLI and gate areas. For reference (they must\nmatch the descriptions in the generated schema):\n\n| Setting | Default | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `limits.deadline` | `10m` | `0s` disables |\n| `limits.request_timeout` | `10m` | |\n| `limits.max_total_tokens` | `3000000` | `0` disables |\n| `limits.max_diff_bytes` | `1000000` | `200000` for `--quick` |\n| `limits.max_prompt_bytes` | `8000000` | |\n| `limits.max_file_ctx_bytes` | `400000` | `100000` for `--quick` |\n| `limits.max_attempts` | `4` | includes the first attempt |\n| `limits.max_verified` | `25` | |\n| `interactive.min_files` | `4` | |\n| `interactive.min_diff_lines` | `80` | |\n| `gate.timeout_seconds` | `180` | `0` disables |\n| `gate.severity` | `HIGH` | compared upper-cased |\n| `gate.offer` | `true` | |\n| `review.snapshot` | `true` | |\n\nA config value overrides the default; a command-line flag (where one\nexists) overrides the config for one invocation (`flag -> config ->\ndefault`). The quick-mode defaults apply only when the config leaves the\nlimit unset.\n\n### 2.2 Config discovery\n\n`findConfig(start, stop)` returns the path of the nearest `.roast.json` at\nor above `start`, never looking above `stop`, or `\"\"` when there is none.\n`stop` must be `start` or an ancestor of it.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n1. `dir = path.resolve(start)` (absolute, symlinks NOT resolved).\n2. `stopCanonical = realpath(path.resolve(stop))`. A failure (for example\n   `stop` does not exist) is an error `find project config: <cause>`.\n3. Loop:\n   1. `candidate = join(dir, \".roast.json\")`. `stat` it (following\n      symlinks). If it exists and is not a directory, return `candidate`\n      (spelled through the non-canonical `dir`). A directory named\n      `.roast.json` is skipped. A stat error other than \"not found\" (for\n      example permission denied) is an error\n      `find project config: <cause>`.\n   2. `canonical = realpath(dir)`; failure is an error as above.\n   3. If `canonical === stopCanonical`, or `dirname(dir) === dir` (the\n      filesystem root), return `\"\"`.\n   4. `dir = dirname(dir)`.\n\nThe comparison uses canonical paths because on macOS a temp directory is\n`/var/...` while `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` prints `/private/var/...`.\nIf `stop` is not an ancestor, the walk ends at the filesystem root.\n\nCLI entry points (module `src/cli/config.ts` in the CLI area, specified\nhere because they are config behavior):\n\n- `projectConfigPath(explicit)`: if `explicit` (the `--config` flag value)\n  is non-empty, return it unchanged (no existence check; `loadConfig`\n  reports a missing file). Otherwise `cwd = process.cwd()` (an error is\n  `find project config: <cause>`), `stop` = Git toplevel of `cwd`\n  (`git -C <cwd> rev-parse --show-toplevel`, trimmed); if that fails\n  (not a repository, or `git` missing), `stop = cwd`. Return\n  `findConfig(cwd, stop)`.\n- `gateConfigPath(repo)`: `findConfig(repo, repo)`. The pre-commit hook\n  runs from the Git toplevel without `--config`, so only a toplevel\n  `.roast.json` counts.\n- `loadProjectConfig(explicit)`: path from `projectConfigPath`; any error\n  is a usage error (exit 2) with the error's message. An empty path is the\n  usage error \"no project config (.roast.json) found between here and the\n  Git toplevel; run `npx roast init` to create one\". A `loadConfig` error\n  is a usage error with its message. On success, attach the stored\n  credential store for the working directory (see 2.6) and return.\n- Usage error for `gate enable` with no toplevel config: \"the pre-commit\n  gate runs from the Git toplevel and finds no .roast.json there; put the\n  project config at the toplevel (`npx roast init`) before enabling the\n  gate\". (Used by the gate area.)\n- No user-level config is ever read (no `~/.config/roast/config.*`); with\n  no project config, a review is a usage error naming `npx roast init`\n  and sends no request.\n\n### 2.3 Loading and parsing\n\n`loadConfig(path)`:\n\n1. Read the file as UTF-8. Failure: `open config <path>: <cause>`.\n2. Decode (below). Failure: `parse config <path>: <cause>`.\n3. Record `dir = path.resolve(dirname(path))` on the config (used by\n   `promptPath`).\n4. Normalize and validate (2.4). These errors are returned WITHOUT the\n   `parse config <path>:` prefix (Go returns them bare).\n\nDecoding, in this order (the first failure wins):\n\n1. Parse JSONC: `//` line comments, `/* */` block comments, and trailing\n   commas in objects and arrays are allowed (the `init` starter writes a\n   trailing comma). Anything else that is not\n   JSON is an error (YAML, unquoted keys, single quotes, empty input).\n2. The document must be exactly one JSON value; trailing content after it\n   (for example a second object) is an error (\"config must contain\n   exactly one JSON object\").\n3. Version header: the value must be an object (an array, string, or\n   number is a type error). Read `version`:\n   - absent or `null`: error `version is required (set \"version\": 1)`.\n   - not an integer number (for example `\"1\"`): type error.\n   - an integer other than 1 (`0`, `2`): error `unsupported config\n     version <n> (this build supports version 1)`.\n4. Literal credential: if `inference_profiles` is an object, and any of\n   its values is an object containing the key `api_key` (any value,\n   including `null`), error `inference_profiles.<name>.api_key is not\n   allowed: a project config never holds a credential; name its\n   environment variable with api_key_env`. This check runs before the\n   unknown-field check so it gives the clearer message, and it applies\n   even when `api_key_env` is also set. If `inference_profiles` is not an\n   object of objects, this step is skipped and step 5 reports the type\n   error.\n5. Strict structural decode against the schema (2.4): unknown keys at any\n   object level are errors naming the key (for example `upload`,\n   `gateway`, `base_url`, `unknown_limit`); every type mismatch is an\n   error.\n\nNull handling (Go `encoding/json` parity): a JSON `null` for any field\nmeans \"unset\" -- the field takes its zero/unset value exactly as if it\nwere absent (`\"limits\": null`, `\"max_output_tokens\": null`,\n`\"snapshot\": null`, `\"pin\": null`, a profile value of `null` are all\naccepted). The exception is `version` (step 3), where `null` is \"required\".\n\nDuplicate keys: the last occurrence wins (same as Go `encoding/json`).\n\nKey case: JSON keys match exactly, case-sensitively (deliberate change 7\nin `spec.md`). Go matched struct keys case-insensitively; here `\"Version\"`,\n`\"INFERENCE_PROFILES\"`, and `\"Model\"` are unknown fields and rejected.\n\nInteger lexemes: an integer field rejects a number whose raw token text is\nnot a plain integer (`1.0`, `1e3`), as Go does (\"Config numbers\" in\n`spec.md`). The check uses the raw token text, not the parsed value.\n\nLone surrogates: validation rejects any string (key or value) containing a\nlone UTF-16 surrogate, so the trust hash is always defined.\n\n### 2.4 Fields of `.roast.json`\n\nTop level (object, unknown keys rejected):\n\n| Key | Type | Required | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `$schema` | string | no | Editor schema reference; ignored. |\n| `version` | integer | yes | Must be `1`. |\n| `inference_profiles` | object of profile | no | Named profiles. |\n| `roles` | object of role | no | `finder` and/or `verifier`. |\n| `review` | object | no | Review defaults. |\n| `prompts` | object | no | Prompt-override paths. |\n| `limits` | object | no | Per-review limits. |\n| `interactive` | object | no | Interactive-mode thresholds. |\n| `gate` | object | no | Pre-commit gate settings. |\n\n`{\"version\": 1}` alone is a valid config. There are no built-in roles or\nmodels: a role absent from `roles` fails only when it is resolved.\n\n#### Inference profile (`inference_profiles.<name>`)\n\nUnknown keys rejected. Every string field treats `\"\"` as unset.\n\n| Key | Type | Default | Validation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `endpoint` | string | protocol default (2.7) | see endpoint rule |\n| `api_key_env` | string | unset | `^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$` |\n| `model` | string | `\"\"` | none at load; checked by `validateRole` |\n| `protocol` | string | `responses` | `chat`, `responses`, `anthropic` |\n| `max_output_tokens` | integer | `32768` | `0`/unset -> default; `< 0` error |\n| `session_header` | string | unset | see session-header rule |\n\nEvery profile is normalized and validated whether or not a role uses it,\nin document order (Go iterates a map, so with several bad profiles the\nreported one is arbitrary; TS reports the first in document order):\n\n1. Name: `name.trim() === \"\"` is an error `inference profile names cannot\n   be empty`.\n2. `protocol` empty -> `responses`. `max_output_tokens` `0` -> `32768`.\n3. `api_key_env` set and not matching the pattern: error\n   `inference_profiles.<name>.api_key_env is not a valid environment\n   variable name`.\n4. Endpoint rule, applied to the effective endpoint (`endpoint`, or the\n   protocol default when unset), with `field = inference_profiles.<name>`\n   (shared with `validateRole`, see 2.9, `validateEndpoint`):\n   - The URL must parse, scheme `http` or `https`, non-empty host, no\n     userinfo (`user:pass@`), no query, no fragment. Else\n     `<field> endpoint must be an http(s) URL without credentials, a\n     query, or a fragment`.\n   - Protocol must be `chat`, `responses`, or `anthropic`. Else\n     `<field> protocol must be chat, responses, or anthropic`.\n   - `max_output_tokens <= 0` (after defaulting, so only a negative\n     value): `<field> max_output_tokens must be positive`.\n5. Session-header rule (only when non-empty), field\n   `inference_profiles.<name>.session_header`:\n   - At most 64 characters and every character an RFC 9110 token\n     character: ASCII letters, digits, or one of\n     ``!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~``. Else `<field> must be an HTTP header name of at\n     most 64 letters, digits, or !#$%&'*+-.^_`|~ characters`.\n   - Not reserved, compared case-insensitively: any name starting with\n     `x-stainless-`, or one of `Authorization`, `X-Api-Key`,\n     `Proxy-Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Host`, `Content-Type`,\n     `Content-Length`, `Content-Encoding`, `Transfer-Encoding`,\n     `Connection`, `Keep-Alive`, `Proxy-Connection`, `TE`, `Trailer`,\n     `Upgrade`, `Expect`, `Accept`, `Accept-Encoding`, `User-Agent`,\n     `X-Agent-Session-Id`, `Anthropic-Version`, `Anthropic-Beta`,\n     `Anthropic-Workspace-Id`, `OpenAI-Organization`, `OpenAI-Project`.\n     Else `<field> cannot be \"<name>\", which is reserved for credentials,\n     request framing, or a header Roast or the provider SDK sets`.\n   - The value is kept as written (case preserved).\n\n#### Role (`roles.<name>`)\n\nKeys of `roles` must be `finder` or `verifier` (case-sensitive); any other\nkey is an error `roles.<name> is not a role (finder or verifier)`. Role\nobject, unknown keys rejected:\n\n| Key | Type | Default | Validation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `profile` | string | required | non-empty; must name a profile |\n| `pin` | boolean | `false` | finder only in effect |\n| `author_routes` | object of string | `{}` | see below |\n\nErrors, in this order per role (roles in document order):\n\n1. `profile` empty or absent: `roles.<name>.profile is required`.\n2. Unknown profile: `roles.<name>.profile references unknown inference\n   profile \"<profile>\"`.\n3. For each `author_routes` entry (document order): normalize the key as\n   `canonicalFamily(key.trim().toLowerCase())` (`gemini` -> `google`).\n   - Normalized key empty: `roles.<name>.author_routes contains empty\n     model family`.\n   - Value not a profile name: `roles.<name>.author_routes.<key>\n     references unknown inference profile \"<value>\"` (`<key>` is the\n     normalized key).\n   - Two keys normalizing to the same value: `roles.<name>.author_routes\n     contains duplicate family \"<key>\"`.\n\n`pin` and `author_routes` are accepted on `verifier` too but have no\neffect there (only finder selection reads them).\n\nKeys of `author_routes` can be either an exact author model ID\n(lower-cased) or a family name; see 2.10.\n\n#### `review`\n\n| Key | Type | Default | Validation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `confidence_floor` | number | `0.6` | `0..1`; `0` -> default |\n| `quick_confidence_floor` | number | `0.8` | `0..1`; `0` -> default |\n| `excludes` | array of string | `[]` | none |\n| `snapshot` | boolean | `true` | none |\n\nAn explicit `0` floor cannot be expressed: it becomes the default. Both\nfloors are validated to `0..1` (deliberate change 20); a value outside the\nrange is an error `<key> must be between 0 and 1 (got <value>)`, checked\nin `normalize` before the floor defaults apply. `excludes` are added\nbefore any `--exclude` flags.\n\n#### `prompts`\n\n| Key | Type | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `finder_code` | string | Finder prompt for code changes |\n| `finder_prose` | string | Finder prompt for prose changes |\n| `verifier` | string | Verifier prompt |\n\n`\"\"` is unset (the embedded template is used). The raw strings are part\nof the trust projection (trust area). Callers read the file through\n`promptPath` (2.8), never the raw string. Contents are used verbatim.\n\n#### `limits`\n\nEvery key is optional; unset means the CLI default (2.1). Optional numbers\ndistinguish an explicit `0` from unset.\n\n| Key | Type | Minimum |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `deadline` | duration string | `>= 0` |\n| `request_timeout` | duration string | `>= 0` |\n| `max_total_tokens` | integer | `0` |\n| `max_diff_bytes` | integer | `1` |\n| `max_prompt_bytes` | integer | `1` |\n| `max_file_ctx_bytes` | integer | `1` |\n| `max_attempts` | integer | `1` |\n| `max_verified` | integer | `0` |\n\n#### `interactive`\n\n| Key | Type | Minimum |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `min_files` | integer | `0` |\n| `min_diff_lines` | integer | `0` |\n\n#### `gate`\n\n| Key | Type | Validation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `timeout_seconds` | integer | `>= 0` |\n| `severity` | string | `INFO`, `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `CRITICAL`, any case |\n| `offer` | boolean | none (`\"no\"` is a type error) |\n\n`severity` is stored as written; consumers upper-case it. `\"\"` is unset.\n\n#### Settings validation order and messages\n\nAfter profiles and roles, defaults for the two confidence floors are\napplied, then settings are checked in this fixed order (first failure\nwins):\n\n1. Integers below their minimum, in the order `limits.max_total_tokens`,\n   `limits.max_diff_bytes`, `limits.max_prompt_bytes`,\n   `limits.max_file_ctx_bytes`, `limits.max_attempts`,\n   `limits.max_verified`, `interactive.min_files`,\n   `interactive.min_diff_lines`, `gate.timeout_seconds`:\n   `<key> must be an integer >= <min> (got <value>)`.\n2. Negative durations, `limits.deadline` then `limits.request_timeout`:\n   `<key> cannot be negative`.\n3. `gate.severity`: `gate.severity must be INFO, LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, or\n   CRITICAL (got \"<value>\")`.\n\nInteger fields reject non-integers (`1.5`), non-integer lexemes (`1.0`,\n`1e3`), and non-numbers as type errors during the structural decode.\n\n#### Duration strings\n\nA duration is a JSON string in Go `time.ParseDuration` syntax, parsed by\nthe shared helper `parseGoDuration` (this module owns it; the CLI area\nuses it for flags):\n\n- Grammar: optional sign `+` or `-`, then one or more `<number><unit>`\n  groups, where `<number>` is digits with an optional fraction (`1`,\n  `1.5`, `.5`, `1.`; at least one digit overall) and `<unit>` is `ns`,\n  `us`, `µs` (U+00B5), `μs` (U+03BC), `ms`, `s`, `m`, `h`. The bare string\n  `0` (optionally signed) is also valid. A missing unit (`\"10\"`) is\n  invalid. Results that overflow a signed 64-bit nanosecond count are\n  invalid.\n- A non-string value: error `duration must be a string such as \"90s\" (got\n  <raw JSON>)`.\n- An unparseable string: error `invalid duration \"<value>\"`.\n- A negative result is rejected by settings validation (`-1s`), but\n  `-0s` is zero and accepted.\n- Represent the value as integer milliseconds or a `bigint` of\n  nanoseconds; Go keeps nanoseconds. Sub-millisecond precision only\n  matters for equality with Go in tests.\n\n### 2.5 Role resolution\n\n`resolveRole(cfg, name, ctx)` expands a role into a `ResolvedRole`:\n\n1. If `roles[name]` is absent or its profile is empty: error `role \"<name>\"\n   is not configured`.\n2. `resolveProfile(name, profileName, selection)`:\n   - Unknown profile: `role <name> references unknown inference profile\n     \"<profile>\"` (unreachable after load validation except via author\n     routes).\n   - Fields: `profile` (name), `baseURL` = effective endpoint,\n     `explicitEndpoint` = profile set `endpoint`, `model`, `protocol`,\n     `maxOutputTokens`, `sessionHeader`, `pin` and `authorRoutes` from the\n     selection, and the attached stored-credential store.\n   - Credential from `resolveCredential` (2.6). An error is wrapped as\n     `role <name>: <cause>`.\n\nOnly the credential comes from outside the config. In particular these\nenvironment variables have NO effect: `ROAST_<ROLE>_BASE_URL`,\n`ROAST_<ROLE>_PROTOCOL`, `ROAST_<ROLE>_MODEL`,\n`ROAST_<ROLE>_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS`, `OPENAI_BASE_URL`, `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL`,\nand any author-model variable.\n\n`ResolvedRole.apiKey` is secret: it must never be copied into run\nmetadata, logs, debug output, or error messages.\n\n### 2.6 Credential resolution\n\n`<ROLE>` below is `name.toUpperCase()`; the role variable is\n`ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`. An environment variable counts only when its value\nis non-empty.\n\n`resolveCredential(roleName, profile, role)`:\n\n1. Role variable `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY` set -> use it,\n   source `{kind: 'role_env', env: 'ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY'}`.\n2. Profile `api_key_env` named:\n   1. That variable set -> use it, source `{kind: 'profile_env', env}`.\n   2. Else, `ambientCredential(role.protocol, role.baseURL, stored)`.\n      If it returns a key, use it with the ambient source. If it errors,\n      return the error. If it returns no key, return no key with source\n      `{kind: 'profile_env', env: <api_key_env>}` (no `withheld`).\n3. Otherwise return `ambientCredential(role.protocol, role.baseURL,\n   stored)`.\n\n`ambientCredential(protocol, endpoint, stored)`:\n\n1. `provider = providerAt(endpoint, protocol)` (2.7). If found:\n   1. `env[provider.keyEnv]` set -> use it, source\n      `{kind: 'provider_env', env: provider.keyEnv}`.\n   2. `storedCredential(stored, protocol, endpoint)`: if it errors,\n      return the error; if it returns a key, use it.\n2. Return no key with source `{kind: 'none', withheld:\n   withheldKey(protocol)}`.\n\n`storedCredential(stored, protocol, endpoint)`: if `providerAt` finds no\nprovider, or no store is attached, return none. Else\n`stored.lookup(provider.name)`; an error is returned as is; an empty key\nis none; else source `{kind: 'stored', provider: provider.name, scope}`.\nThe registry alone decides the provider, so a custom endpoint never\nreceives a stored key.\n\n`withheldKey(protocol)`: candidates, in order, are the standard key\nvariable of the provider at `defaultBaseURL(protocol)` (`OPENAI_API_KEY`\nfor `responses`/`chat` and any unknown protocol, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for\n`anthropic`), then every registry provider's `keyEnv` in registry order\n(`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`). Return the\nfirst one that is set, else `\"\"`. Because it is called only after no key\nwas sent, any variable it names was withheld.\n\nResulting precedence (for the default store):\n\n1. `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`\n2. the profile's `api_key_env`\n3. the built-in provider's standard variable (only on that provider's\n   endpoint with a protocol it accepts there)\n4. the provider's project-scope stored credential (same condition)\n5. the provider's user-scope stored credential (same condition)\n\nSteps 4 and 5 are inside the store (`lookup` returns project before\nuser); see the trust-and-credentials area.\n\nStored-credential interface (implemented by the credentials module, in\nthe trust-and-credentials area):\n\n```ts\nexport interface StoredCredentials {\n    // '' key when none is stored. Rejects (throws) when a credentials\n    // file cannot be used; it never skips a bad file.\n    lookup(provider:string):{key:string, scope:'project'|'user'|''}\n}\n```\n\nThe store is read lazily: a broken credentials file fails resolution only\nwhen a role actually reaches step 4 (no environment credential first).\nThe CLI attaches `credentials.newStore('.')` to every config it loads\n(`withStoredCredentials`); any new code path that resolves roles from a\nloaded config must attach it too.\n\n`CredentialSource`:\n\n```ts\ntype CredentialKind =\n    'role_env'|'profile_env'|'provider_env'|'stored'|'none'\ninterface CredentialSource {\n    kind:CredentialKind\n    env?:string       // variable read, for env kinds\n    provider?:string  // stored: provider name\n    scope?:string     // stored: 'project' or 'user'\n    withheld?:string  // none: a set provider key that was not sent\n}\n```\n\nIts human-readable form (`describeSource`), never containing a secret:\n\n| Kind | Text |\n| --- | --- |\n| `role_env` | `<env> (role override)` |\n| `profile_env` | `<env> (profile api_key_env)` |\n| `provider_env` | `<env> (ambient provider key)` |\n| `stored` | `stored <scope> credential for <provider>` |\n| `none`, withheld | see below |\n| `none` | `none` |\n\nThe withheld form is `none; <withheld> is set but is sent only to its\nprovider's default endpoint`.\n\n`credentialEnvNames(cfg)`: every non-empty `api_key_env` across all\nprofiles (duplicates allowed), so run-record capture can always redact\nthem (record area).\n\n### 2.7 Built-in providers\n\nRegistry, in this order (order matters for `withheldKey` and listings):\n\n1. `openai`\n   - endpoint `https://api.openai.com/v1`\n   - protocols `responses`, `chat` (first = the one starter profiles use)\n   - keyEnv `OPENAI_API_KEY`\n   - keyPage `https://platform.openai.com/api-keys`\n   - login `paste`\n2. `anthropic`\n   - endpoint `https://api.anthropic.com/v1`\n   - protocols `anthropic`\n   - keyEnv `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`\n   - keyPage `https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys`\n   - login `paste`\n3. `openrouter`\n   - endpoint `https://openrouter.ai/api/v1`\n   - protocols `chat`, `responses`\n   - keyEnv `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`\n   - keyPage `https://openrouter.ai/settings/keys`\n   - login `browser`\n\nLogin methods: `paste` opens the key page and reads a pasted key;\n`browser` runs a browser key exchange (OpenRouter PKCE, login area).\n\nInvariant: no two entries share an (endpoint, protocol) pair, so\n`providerAt` finds at most one.\n\n- `builtinProviders()`: copies of the registry (callers cannot mutate\n  it).\n- `lookupProvider(name)`: the provider with exactly this name\n  (case-sensitive), or undefined.\n- `providerAt(endpoint, protocol)`: the first provider that accepts\n  `protocol` and whose endpoint `sameEndpoint`s `endpoint`, or undefined.\n- `defaultBaseURL(protocol)`: `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` for\n  `anthropic`, otherwise `https://api.openai.com/v1` (including unknown\n  protocols). OpenRouter is never a protocol's implicit default. A\n  profile that omits `endpoint` uses this.\n\n`sameEndpoint(a, b)`: trim both and parse as URLs; if either fails to\nparse or has an empty host, false. Otherwise equal when scheme matches\ncase-insensitively, host (including port) matches case-insensitively,\npaths match after stripping trailing `/` characters, raw query strings\nmatch exactly, and userinfo matches exactly.\n\nExamples (`providerAt`): `https://api.openai.com/v1/` + `chat` ->\nopenai; `HTTPS://API.OPENAI.COM/v1` + `responses` -> openai;\n`https://api.openai.com/v1` + `anthropic` -> none;\n`https://api.anthropic.com/v1` + `chat` -> none; OpenRouter + `responses`\n-> openrouter; OpenRouter + `anthropic` -> none; `http://api.openai.com/v1`\n-> none; `https://api.openai.com/v2` -> none; `\"\"` -> none.\n\nConsumers: trust standardness (trust area: a profile on a built-in\nprovider's endpoint, with an accepted protocol and the standard key\nvariable, needs no approval), ambient credentials (2.6), the `init`\nstarter (doctor/init area), `login`/`logout` provider lookup and\n`providersNeedingLogin` (login area), and the credentials file's provider\nvalidation. Adding a provider means adding a registry row only.\n\n### 2.8 Prompt paths\n\n`promptPath(cfg, p)`: `\"\"` stays `\"\"`; an absolute path is returned as\nis; if the config has no recorded `dir` (not loaded from a file) the path\nis returned as is; otherwise `path.join(cfg.dir, p)` (normalized). So the\nsame file is read wherever in the repository Roast runs. The trust\nprojection hashes the raw string, so approvals survive worktree moves.\n\n### 2.9 Endpoint overrides (`--<role>-base-url`, `--<role>-protocol`)\n\n`overrideEndpoint(roleName, role, baseURL?, protocol?)` applies the\noverrides, then re-binds the credential. `undefined` means the flag was\nnot given (an explicitly given empty string is still an override). It\nfails only when a stored credential the role now needs cannot be read.\nThe CLI calls it only when at least one of the two flags was given, then\napplies `--<role>-model` and `--<role>-max-output-tokens` directly.\n\n`applyEndpointOverrides(role, baseURL?, protocol?)` returns\n`crossedProvider`:\n\n1. `crossed = false`.\n2. If `protocol` given: `previous = defaultBaseURL(role.protocol)`;\n   `role.protocol = protocol`; `next = defaultBaseURL(protocol)`. If\n   `next !== previous`:\n   - if `!role.explicitEndpoint`: `role.baseURL = next`, `crossed = true`;\n   - else if `sameEndpoint(role.baseURL, previous)`: `role.baseURL =\n     next`, `crossed = true` (`explicitEndpoint` stays true);\n   - else the explicit custom endpoint stays.\n3. If `baseURL` given: `role.baseURL = baseURL`, `explicitEndpoint =\n   true`, `crossed = false`.\n\n`rebindCredential(roleName, role, crossed)`:\n\n1. `role_env`: keep the credential.\n2. `profile_env` and not `crossed`: keep it (even when its key is empty).\n   A profile credential follows an explicit endpoint override.\n3. Otherwise: if `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY` is set, use it (`role_env`).\n   Else `ambientCredential(role.protocol, role.baseURL, stored)`; an\n   error becomes `role <name>: <cause>`.\n\nConsequences (pinned by tests):\n\nEach item reads \"start; override -> endpoint, key\". \"Implicit\" means\nthe profile sets no `endpoint`; \"profile key\" is the `api_key_env` value.\n\n1. implicit, `api_key_env` set; protocol `anthropic` -> Anthropic\n   default, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`.\n2. explicit `https://api.openai.com/v1/`, `api_key_env`; protocol\n   `anthropic` -> Anthropic default, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`.\n3. explicit custom endpoint, `api_key_env`; protocol `anthropic` ->\n   unchanged endpoint, profile key.\n4. implicit, `api_key_env`; protocol `chat` -> OpenAI default, profile\n   key.\n5. implicit, `api_key_env`; protocol `anthropic` and a base URL -> the\n   chosen URL, profile key.\n6. OpenRouter chat with `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`; base URL -> chosen URL, no\n   key.\n7. OpenRouter chat; protocol `responses` -> OpenRouter,\n   `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`.\n8. OpenRouter chat; protocol `anthropic` -> OpenRouter, no key.\n9. implicit with `OPENAI_API_KEY`; base URL -> chosen URL, no key.\n10. `anthropic` protocol, only an anthropic stored key; custom base URL\n    -> no key.\n11. implicit `anthropic`, only an anthropic stored key; protocol\n    `responses` -> OpenAI default, no key.\n12. explicit Anthropic default, only an anthropic stored key; protocol\n    `chat` -> OpenAI default, no key.\n13. implicit `anthropic`, stored keys for every provider; protocol\n    `responses` -> the project-scope OpenAI stored key.\n14. OpenRouter chat, unset `api_key_env` value, `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` set;\n    protocol `responses` -> same key before and after.\n\nInitial resolution (`resolveCredential`) and re-binding must agree: both\nreach the provider variable and the stored key only through\n`ambientCredential`, so a role picks the same credential with or without\na same-provider protocol override.\n\n### 2.10 Model families and finder flip\n\n`inferFamily(model)`: provider-neutral family, or `\"\"`.\n\n1. `m = model.trim().toLowerCase()`; empty -> `\"\"`.\n2. If `m` contains `/`: split at the first `/`. If the prefix is one of\n   `anthropic`, `openai`, `google`, `gemini`, `mistral`, `xai`, `cohere`,\n   `deepseek`, return `canonicalFamily(prefix)`. Otherwise `m` = the part\n   after the first `/`.\n3. Strip a trailing `:api`, then a trailing `[1m]` (in that order, each\n   at most once).\n4. Match, first rule wins:\n   - `anthropic`, or prefix `claude-` -> `anthropic`\n   - `openai`, or prefix `gpt-`, `codex-`, `chatgpt-`, `o1`, `o3`, `o4`\n     -> `openai`\n   - `google`, `gemini`, or prefix `gemini-` -> `google`\n   - `mistral`, or prefix `mistral-`, `codestral-` -> `mistral`\n   - `xai`, or prefix `grok-` -> `xai`\n   - `cohere`, or prefix `command-` -> `cohere`\n   - `deepseek`, or prefix `deepseek-` -> `deepseek`\n   - else `\"\"`.\n\n`canonicalFamily(f)`: `gemini` -> `google`, else `f`.\n\nExamples: `anthropic/claude-sonnet` -> anthropic; `gpt-5-codex` -> openai;\n`codex-mini-latest` -> openai; `openai/gpt-6.1-terra` -> openai;\n`google/gemini-2.5-pro` -> google; `grok-4` -> xai;\n`meta-llama/llama-4:free` -> `\"\"`.\n\n`resolveFinderFlip(cfg, finder, authorModel, ctx)` returns\n`{role, note?, warn?}` (the CLI prints them; wording in the CLI area may be\nequivalent):\n\n1. `authorKey = authorModel.trim().toLowerCase()`; empty -> return the\n   finder unchanged.\n2. `authorFamily = inferFamily(authorModel)`.\n3. If `finder.pin`: keep the finder; if `authorFamily !== ''` and\n   `inferFamily(finder.model) === authorFamily`, warn \"author-model <a>\n   shares the finder family <f>, but the finder is pinned; generation\n   independence is reduced\".\n4. `target = finder.authorRoutes[authorKey]`, else (when `authorFamily`\n   is non-empty) `finder.authorRoutes[authorFamily]`.\n5. No target: keep the finder. If `authorFamily === ''` warn \"could not\n   determine a model family for author-model \"<a>\" and no exact author\n   route is configured; keeping finder <model>\"; else if the finder's\n   family equals `authorFamily` warn \"author-model <a> shares the finder\n   family <f> and no author route is configured; generation independence\n   is reduced\".\n6. Target found: `resolved = resolveProfile('finder', target, {})` (full\n   credential resolution under the `finder` role variable; `pin` false,\n   no routes). An error is returned.\n7. `targetFamily = inferFamily(resolved.model)`. If (`authorFamily` is\n   non-empty and (`targetFamily` is empty or equals `authorFamily`)) or\n   `resolved.model` equals `authorModel` case-insensitively: keep the\n   original finder, warn \"author-model <a> shares the finder family <f>\n   and profile \"<target>\" uses non-independent model \"<m>\"; keeping\n   finder <model>\".\n8. Else use `resolved`, with note \"author-model <a> matched an exact\n   route; using finder profile <target> (<model>)\" when `authorFamily` is\n   empty, or \"author-model <a> maps to family <f>; using finder profile\n   <target> (<model>)\".\n\nAn author-route profile follows the same credential rules (2.6): a route\nto a custom endpoint without `api_key_env` gets no ambient key.\n\n### 2.11 Role validation before any model call\n\n`isPlaceholderModel(model)`: after trimming, length > 2, starts with `<`\nand ends with `>` (the starter uses `<finder-model>` and\n`<verifier-model>`; defined with the starter in the doctor/init area, used\nhere).\n\n`validateRole(name, role)`, first failure wins:\n\n1. `role.model.trim() === ''`: `<name> model is not configured (set\n   inference_profiles.<profile>.model or --<name>-model)`.\n2. Placeholder model: `<name> model <model> is a placeholder: set\n   inference_profiles.<profile>.model in the project config to a model\n   ID, or pass --<name>-model`.\n3. `role.apiKey.trim() === ''`, with `provider = providerAt(role.baseURL,\n   role.protocol)`:\n   - built-in and source `profile_env`: `<name> API key is not configured\n     (run `npx roast login <provider>`, or set <env>, named by\n     inference_profiles.<profile>.api_key_env, or <roleVar>)`\n   - built-in: `<name> API key is not configured (run `npx roast login\n     <provider>`, or set <keyEnv> or <roleVar>)`\n   - source `profile_env`: `<name> API key is not configured (set <env>,\n     named by inference_profiles.<profile>.api_key_env, or <roleVar>)`\n   - `withheld` set: `<name> API key is not configured: <withheld> is not\n     sent to <baseURL> because it is not that provider's default endpoint\n     (set inference_profiles.<profile>.api_key_env or <roleVar>)`\n   - else: `<name> API key is not configured (set\n     inference_profiles.<profile>.api_key_env or <roleVar>)`\n4. `validateEndpoint(name, role.baseURL, role.protocol,\n   role.maxOutputTokens)` (2.4 endpoint rule, with field = role name).\n   This is where a flag-supplied protocol, endpoint, or output limit is\n   validated.\n\nThe CLI turns these into usage errors before any request (CLI area);\n`doctor` reports them per role (doctor area).\n\n### 2.12 Unported Go helpers\n\n`DescribeCredential`, `IsLoopbackHost` (localhost or a loopback IP,\ntrailing `.` ignored), and `InferenceProfile.DescribeEndpoint` have no\nnon-test callers at the baseline. They are not ported (deliberate change\n23 in `spec.md`); `doctor` uses `describeSource` instead.\n\n## 3. TypeScript module design\n\nAll modules live under `src/config/`; tests under `test/config/`. Config\ndata keeps the JSON key spelling (snake_case) so the zod schema's inferred\ntype is the config type; runtime role objects use camelCase.\n\n### Parsing pipeline\n\n`decode(text)` is staged so the Go error order (2.3) holds:\n\n1. `parseJsonc(text)` (`src/config/jsonc.ts`): `jsonc-parser`'s\n   `parseTree` with `allowTrailingComma: true` (the `init` starter writes\n   a trailing comma) and comments allowed. Any parse error, including\n   `EndOfFileExpected` for trailing content, is fatal. Convert the tree\n   into plain values whose objects have a null prototype, assigning keys\n   with `Object.defineProperty` so a key named `__proto__` (a legal\n   profile name in Go) is an ordinary key; a duplicate key keeps the last\n   value (same as Go). Keys are kept as written and matched exactly, so\n   `\"Version\"` is an unknown field. A `visit` pass (jsonc-parser's\n   visitor) records the raw token text of every number; a number whose\n   lexeme is not a plain integer (`1.0`, `1e3`) is flagged by path, and\n   the schema step reports a type error if that path is an integer\n   field.\n2. `checkVersion(value)`: not a plain object -> type error; `version`\n   absent or `null` -> required; not an integer -> type error; not 1 ->\n   unsupported.\n3. `rejectLiteralCredentials(value)`.\n4. `nullsAsUnset(value)`: Go parity for `null` (2.3). In fixed-shape\n   objects a `null` property is deleted. In the map-shaped objects a\n   `null` value becomes the zero value: an `inference_profiles` or `roles`\n   entry becomes `{}`, an `author_routes` entry becomes `\"\"`. A `null`\n   element of `review.excludes` becomes `\"\"`.\n5. `rejectLoneSurrogates(value)`: any key or string value containing a\n   lone surrogate is an error, so the trust hash is always defined.\n   Then `ConfigSchema.safeParse(value)`: strict structure and types. Zod\n   issues map to messages `<dotted.path>: <reason>`, with unknown keys\n   reported as `unknown field \"<key>\"` at their path. Wording need only be\n   equivalent to Go's.\n6. `normalize(raw, dir)`: profile defaults and checks (2.4), role checks,\n   confidence-floor defaults, then `validateSettings`. These run as plain\n   functions rather than zod refinements so their order and messages are\n   explicit and so the JSON Schema generator never sees them.\n\n### `src/config/schema.ts`\n\nThe zod (v4) schema is the single source of truth for shape, types, and\nthe editor schema. Every field carries `.meta({description})` with the\ndescription text currently in `roast.schema.json` (defaults included).\n\n```ts\nimport {z} from 'zod'\n\nexport const Duration = z.string()\n    .refine(isGoDuration, 'invalid duration')\n    .meta({\n        pattern: '^([0-9]+(\\\\.[0-9]+)?(ns|us|µs|ms|s|m|h))+$|^0$',\n        description: 'A Go duration such as \"90s\" or \"10m\".'\n    })\n\nexport const InferenceProfile = z.strictObject({\n    endpoint: z.string().optional(),       // format: uri in meta\n    api_key_env: z.string()\n        .regex(/^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$/).optional(),\n    model: z.string().optional(),\n    protocol: z.enum(['responses', 'chat', 'anthropic']).optional(),\n    max_output_tokens: z.int().optional(),\n    session_header: z.string().optional(),\n})\n// ...Role, Review, Prompts, Limits, Interactive, Gate likewise\nexport const ConfigSchema = z.strictObject({\n    $schema: z.string().optional(),\n    version: z.literal(1),\n    inference_profiles: z.record(z.string(), InferenceProfile).optional(),\n    roles: z.strictObject({\n        finder: Role.optional(),\n        verifier: Role.optional(),\n    }).optional(),\n    // review, prompts, limits, interactive, gate\n})\nexport type RawConfig = z.infer<typeof ConfigSchema>\n```\n\nSchema-vs-runtime rules:\n\n- Where Go accepts an empty string as \"unset\" but the editor schema shows\n  a constraint (`api_key_env` pattern, `protocol` enum), the zod check\n  must accept `\"\"` too (for example `.or(z.literal(''))`) while the\n  generated JSON Schema keeps the constraint. Use zod 4's\n  `toJSONSchema` `override` hook, or metadata, for every place where the\n  editor schema is intentionally stricter than or different from the\n  runtime check. Each such place is listed in a comment next to the\n  field. They are: `protocol` and `api_key_env` (`\"\"` accepted),\n  `gate.severity` (runtime accepts any letter case, schema shows the\n  upper-case enum), `review.confidence_floor` and\n  `quick_confidence_floor` (schema shows `minimum: 0`, `maximum: 1`;\n  runtime checks the same range in `normalize`), `max_output_tokens`\n  (schema `minimum: 1`,\n  runtime accepts `0` as default and rejects negatives in `normalize`),\n  `session_header` (schema `maxLength: 64` only), and the integer\n  minimums (schema `minimum`, runtime checks in `validateSettings` so\n  the messages and order match 2.4).\n- Roles under `roles` other than `finder`/`verifier` produce the specific\n  message `roles.<name> is not a role (finder or verifier)`, not a\n  generic unknown-key message.\n\n### Schema generation\n\n`scripts/gen-schema.ts` (bundled with esbuild like the tests, then run by\nNode) builds the editor schema and writes `roast.schema.json` (2-space\nindent, trailing newline):\n\n```ts\nconst schema = {\n    $schema: 'http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#',\n    $id: 'https://github.com/nichoth/roast/roast.schema.json',\n    title: 'Roast project config (.roast.json)',\n    description: 'Every non-secret Roast setting. ...',\n    ...z.toJSONSchema(ConfigSchema, {\n        target: 'draft-7', io: 'input', override: editorOverrides\n    })\n}\n```\n\nThe module exports `renderProjectSchema():string` (in\n`src/config/schema.ts`), which returns the full file text; the packaging\narea's build calls it to generate `roast.schema.json`, and\n`scripts/gen-schema.ts` is a thin wrapper around it. npm script\n`build:schema` runs it. The test `test/config/schema.ts`\ngenerates the schema in memory and asserts it deep-equals the committed\nfile (parsed JSON, not text), failing with \"run npm run build:schema\".\nThis replaces Go's reflection-based drift test. The file stays in\n`package.json` `files`, and the starter's `$schema` value stays\n`./node_modules/@substrate-system/roast/roast.schema.json`.\n\n### `src/config/load.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport const PROJECT_CONFIG_NAME = '.roast.json'\nexport const CURRENT_VERSION = 1\n\nexport interface ProjectConfig extends NormalizedConfig {\n    readonly dir:string            // '' when not loaded from a file\n    readonly stored?:StoredCredentials\n}\nexport function findConfig(start:string, stop:string):string\nexport function loadConfig(path:string):ProjectConfig\nexport function decode(text:string):RawConfig\nexport function normalize(raw:RawConfig, dir:string):ProjectConfig\nexport function withStoredCredentials(\n    cfg:ProjectConfig,\n    store:StoredCredentials\n):ProjectConfig\nexport function promptPath(cfg:ProjectConfig, p:string):string\nexport function credentialEnvNames(cfg:ProjectConfig):string[]\nexport function snapshotEnabled(cfg:ProjectConfig):boolean\nexport function gateOffers(cfg:ProjectConfig):boolean\n```\n\n`NormalizedConfig` is `RawConfig` with `inference_profiles` and `roles`\nalways present (possibly empty), every profile's `protocol` and\n`max_output_tokens` filled, `author_routes` keys normalized, and both\nconfidence floors filled. Optional numbers stay `number|undefined` so an\nexplicit `0` differs from unset; `limits.deadline` and\n`limits.request_timeout` are stored parsed (`GoDuration`, below).\n\nErrors are thrown as `ConfigError` (a plain `Error` subclass); the CLI\nwraps them as usage errors. Filesystem calls are synchronous (`node:fs`),\nwhich keeps `loadConfig` and `resolveRole` synchronous.\n\n### `src/config/duration.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport type GoDuration = bigint            // nanoseconds\nexport function parseGoDuration(s:string):GoDuration  // throws\nexport function isGoDuration(s:string):boolean\nexport function toMillis(d:GoDuration):number\n```\n\nShared with the CLI area for `--deadline` and `--request-timeout`.\n\n### `src/config/provider.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport type Protocol = 'chat'|'responses'|'anthropic'\nexport type LoginMethod = 'paste'|'browser'\nexport interface BuiltinProvider {\n    readonly name:string\n    readonly endpoint:string\n    readonly protocols:readonly Protocol[]\n    readonly keyEnv:string\n    readonly keyPage:string\n    readonly login:LoginMethod\n}\nexport function builtinProviders():BuiltinProvider[]\nexport function lookupProvider(name:string):BuiltinProvider|undefined\nexport function providerAt(\n    endpoint:string,\n    protocol:string\n):BuiltinProvider|undefined\nexport function defaultBaseURL(protocol:string):string\nexport function sameEndpoint(a:string, b:string):boolean\n```\n\nURL parsing uses the WHATWG `URL` class. Only the empty-host case is\npinned to Go; other odd hosts (spaces, IDNA) follow WHATWG. A value whose text\nafter the scheme does not start with `//` plus a non-empty authority (for\nexample `https:///v1` or `https:v1`) is treated as having an empty host\n(WHATWG would otherwise repair it), matching Go. Endpoint strings are\nstored and sent\nexactly as written; parsing is only for checks and comparison.\n\n### `src/config/credential.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport type Env = Readonly<Record<string, string|undefined>>\nexport interface StoredCredentials {\n    lookup(provider:string):{key:string, scope:string}\n}\nexport type CredentialKind =\n    'role_env'|'profile_env'|'provider_env'|'stored'|'none'\nexport interface CredentialSource { /* 2.6 */ }\nexport function describeSource(s:CredentialSource):string\nexport function roleKeyVar(role:string):string   // ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY\nexport function ambientCredential(\n    protocol:string,\n    endpoint:string,\n    stored:StoredCredentials|undefined,\n    env:Env\n):{key:string, source:CredentialSource}\nexport function overrideEndpoint(\n    roleName:string,\n    role:ResolvedRole,\n    overrides:{baseURL?:string, protocol?:string},\n    env:Env\n):ResolvedRole\n```\n\n`env` is passed explicitly everywhere (never read from `process.env`\ninside this area) so the in-process CLI seam can inject it.\n`rebindCredential` and `applyEndpointOverrides` are module-private but\nexported from an `internal` object for the matrix tests.\n\n### `src/config/role.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport interface ResolvedRole {\n    readonly profile:string\n    readonly baseURL:string\n    readonly apiKey:string            // secret; never logged or recorded\n    readonly model:string\n    readonly protocol:string\n    readonly maxOutputTokens:number\n    readonly sessionHeader:string\n    readonly pin:boolean\n    readonly authorRoutes:Readonly<Record<string, string>>\n    readonly credential:CredentialSource\n    readonly explicitEndpoint:boolean\n    readonly stored?:StoredCredentials\n}\nexport function resolveRole(\n    cfg:ProjectConfig,\n    name:'finder'|'verifier',\n    env:Env\n):ResolvedRole\nexport function resolveFinderFlip(\n    cfg:ProjectConfig,\n    finder:ResolvedRole,\n    authorModel:string,\n    env:Env\n):{role:ResolvedRole, note?:string, warn?:string}\nexport function validateRole(name:string, role:ResolvedRole):void\nexport function validateEndpoint(\n    field:string,\n    baseURL:string,\n    protocol:string,\n    maxOutputTokens:number\n):void\n```\n\n`ResolvedRole` objects are immutable; overrides return a new object.\n`apiKey` must be excluded from any `toJSON`/debug rendering: give\n`ResolvedRole` no enumerable path into run metadata, and debug output in\nthis area logs only the credential source kind and variable name.\n\n### `src/config/family.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport function inferFamily(model:string):string\nexport function canonicalFamily(family:string):string\n```\n\n### Dependencies\n\n- Uses: `jsonc-parser`, `zod`, `node:fs`, `node:path`,\n  `@substrate-system/debug` (namespace `roast:config`).\n- Used by: CLI (load, flags, resolution, validation), trust (projection\n  uses `providerAt`, the effective endpoint, `promptPath` raw strings),\n  credentials (`lookupProvider`), login/logout (`builtinProviders`,\n  `lookupProvider`, `providerAt`), doctor (`validateRole`,\n  `describeSource`), init starter (`inferFamily`, `lookupProvider`,\n  registry protocol order), record (`credentialEnvNames`), gate\n  (`findConfig` via `gateConfigPath`, `gate` settings).\n- The Git toplevel lookup in `projectConfigPath` comes from the scope\n  area's `gitRoot` helper.\n- `src/config` must not import the credentials module (the store is\n  injected through the `StoredCredentials` interface), mirroring Go.\n\n## 4. Deliberate changes\n\n- Node `>=22.18`, darwin/linux only: no Windows path handling in\n  `findConfig` or `promptPath`.\n- Debug tracing under `roast:config` (`DEBUG=roast:*`): may log the\n  config path found, the profile chosen per role, and the credential\n  source kind and variable name; never a key, header, or prompt.\n- Validation messages need only be equivalent to Go's, not identical.\n- Order of errors among several invalid profiles or roles is document\n  order (Go's was arbitrary map order).\n- JSON keys match exactly, case-sensitively (deliberate change 7): Go\n  accepted `\"Version\"`; here it is an unknown field. `null` still means\n  absent.\n- `review.confidence_floor` and `quick_confidence_floor` are validated to\n  `0..1` (deliberate change 20); Go did not range-check them.\n- `DescribeCredential`, `IsLoopbackHost`, and `DescribeEndpoint` are not\n  ported (deliberate change 23).\n- Strings containing lone surrogates are rejected, so the trust hash is\n  always defined (\"Bytes and text\" in `spec.md`).\n- Endpoint URL parsing is WHATWG; only the empty-host case is pinned to\n  Go.\n\nNo other change: every field, default, range, and error condition of\n`.roast.json` version 1 is kept.\n\n## 5. Test plan\n\nSeams: config loading and validation are observable through the CLI at\nlow cost (exit code 2, no request reaches the fake server), so the\nrepresentative cases run at the CLI seam. The credential and override\nmatrices (about 100 combinations) and pure functions (`inferFamily`,\n`providerAt`, duration parsing) run at the module seam, because each CLI\ncase would need a full review against the fake server to observe one\n`Authorization` header. A handful of credential cases also run at the CLI\nseam to prove the wiring (which key reaches which endpoint).\n\n### Discovery (CLI seam unless noted)\n\n- Nearest config wins; nothing above the stop directory is found; a\n  config at the stop directory is found (`TestFindWalksUpNoFurtherThanStop`,\n  module seam).\n- Review in a subdirectory uses the nearest config below the Git\n  toplevel (`TestReviewUsesNearestProjectConfigBelowGitToplevel`).\n- `--config` overrides discovery\n  (`TestConfigFlagOverridesDiscoveredProjectConfig`).\n- No config: usage error, no request\n  (`TestReviewWithoutProjectConfigNamesRoastInit`); a user-level config\n  directory is never read (`TestUserConfigDirectoryIsNotRead`).\n- Symlinked working directory vs `realpath` toplevel still stops at the\n  toplevel (new; covers the macOS `/private/var` case).\n\n### Parsing and validation\n\n- JSONC comments and trailing commas accepted\n  (`TestLoadAcceptsJSONCCommentsAndTrailingCommas`,\n  `TestReviewUsesModelsFromJSONCProjectConfig`).\n- Rejected: unknown top-level field, unknown profile field (`base_url`),\n  missing/zero/future/string version, literal `api_key`, trailing\n  document, non-object, YAML (`TestLoadRejectsInvalidProjectConfig`);\n  literal `api_key` even beside `api_key_env`\n  (`TestLoadRejectsProfileCredentialAmbiguity`); `upload` section\n  (`TestLoadRejectsUploadSection`); missing file\n  (`TestLoadMissingFileIsAnError`); via the CLI as exit 2 with no request\n  (`TestReviewRejectsInvalidProjectConfig`).\n- Session header: valid value kept; too long, bad characters, and every\n  reserved name in any case rejected (`TestLoadSessionHeader`,\n  `TestLoadRejectsInvalidSessionHeader`).\n- Limits: `max_diff_bytes: 0`, `max_attempts: 0`, `max_total_tokens: -1`,\n  `request_timeout: \"later\"`, `deadline: \"-1s\"`, unknown limit rejected\n  (`TestInvalidLimitsAreRejected`); limits from config bound the review\n  (`TestLimitsInProjectConfigBoundTheReview`,\n  `TestMaxDiffBytesInProjectConfigTruncatesTheDiff`,\n  `TestMaxPromptBytesInProjectConfigRejectsALargePrompt`); flags win for\n  one invocation (`TestFlagsOverrideProjectConfigForOneInvocation`);\n  interactive thresholds and `review.snapshot` honored\n  (`TestInteractiveThresholdsInProjectConfig`,\n  `TestReviewSnapshotSettingInProjectConfig`).\n- Gate: `severity: \"SEVERE\"`, `timeout_seconds: -1`, `offer: \"no\"`\n  rejected (`TestInvalidGateSettingsAreRejected`); settings come from the\n  config or default (`TestGateSettingsComeFromTheProjectConfig`,\n  `TestGateSettingsDefaultWhenUnset`); `offer: false` stops the offer\n  (`TestGateOfferFalseStopsTheGateOffer`).\n- New parity cases (module seam): `null` fields act as unset; a profile\n  named `__proto__` loads as an ordinary profile; lower-case\n  `gate.severity` accepted; author-route keys `\" Gemini \"` and `google`\n  collide as duplicates; `max_output_tokens: 0` becomes 32768 and `-1`\n  is rejected.\n- Tightenings (module seam): `\"Version\": 1` and `\"Model\"` in a profile\n  are rejected as unknown fields; a duplicate key keeps the last value;\n  `max_attempts: 1.0` and `1e3` are rejected as non-integer lexemes while\n  `1` loads; a trailing comma after the last property loads;\n  `confidence_floor: 1.5` and `-0.1` are rejected, `0`, `0.5`, `1`\n  accepted; a string containing a lone surrogate (written as an escape\n  in the JSON) is rejected.\n- Duration parser: Go grammar accept/reject table, including `0`, `.5s`,\n  `1h30m`, `µs`/`μs`, `+5s`, `-0s`, and rejects `10`, `\"\"`, `1x`.\n\n### Schema\n\n- `renderProjectSchema()` output, parsed, deep-equals the committed\n  `roast.schema.json` (replaces `TestSchemaMatchesConfigStruct`).\n- Every example config used by tests and the `init` starter validates\n  against the generated JSON Schema (new; cheap guard that the editor\n  schema is not stricter than intended for real configs).\n\n### Resolution and credentials (module seam, plus CLI wiring)\n\n- Named profiles resolve with defaults; Anthropic defaults for the\n  `anthropic` protocol (`TestLoadResolvesNamedProfiles`,\n  `TestResolveRoleUsesNativeAnthropicDefaults`).\n- Legacy environment overrides are ignored\n  (`TestResolveRoleIgnoresLegacyGlobalOverrides`,\n  `TestRemovedRoleEnvironmentVariablesAreIgnored`,\n  `TestRemovedEnvironmentVariablesHaveNoEffect` at the CLI seam).\n- Precedence matrix: protocol x endpoint (unset, default, custom,\n  ambient gateway) x `api_key_env` x `*_BASE_URL` set x role key; checks\n  endpoint, key, source kind, and `withheld`\n  (`TestCredentialAndEndpointPrecedence`).\n- `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` only at OpenRouter with chat/responses, withheld\n  elsewhere (`TestOpenRouterKeyOnlyAtOpenRouter`).\n- Custom endpoint without a named credential gets no ambient key, and\n  validation names the withheld variable and the role variable\n  (`TestTemplateProfileWithoutCredentialNeverReceivesAmbientKey`).\n- Trailing slash on the default endpoint still matches\n  (`TestEndpointComparisonIgnoresATrailingSlash`).\n- Author-route profiles follow the same credential rule\n  (`TestAuthorRouteProfileUsesTheSameCredentialRule`).\n- Override table in 2.9 (`TestEndpointFlagOverrides`,\n  `TestRebindCredential`, `TestStoredCredentialDoesNotFollowAnOverride`,\n  `TestUnsetProfileVariableOnABuiltinEndpointPrefersProviderVariable`).\n- Stored credentials: used at a built-in endpoint; ranked below every\n  env variable, project before user; never sent to a custom endpoint;\n  an unusable store fails resolution only when reached; the source names\n  scope and provider, never the key\n  (`TestStoredCredentialAtABuiltinEndpoint`,\n  `TestStoredCredentialPrecedence`,\n  `TestStoredCredentialNeverReachesACustomEndpoint`,\n  `TestUnusableStoredCredentialsFailResolution`,\n  `TestStoredCredentialSourceNamesScopeNotSecret`).\n- No source rendering ever contains a secret\n  (`TestCredentialSourceNeverIncludesSecret`).\n- Missing credential on a built-in provider suggests login\n  (`TestMissingCredentialOnABuiltinProviderSuggestsLogin`); the\n  `describeCredential` test is not ported.\n- Embedded userinfo in an endpoint fails validation\n  (`TestValidateRoleRejectsEmbeddedCredentials`).\n- CLI wiring: shell provider key reaches only its default endpoint; not a\n  custom endpoint; a named credential and a role key work on a custom\n  endpoint (`TestShellProviderKeyGoesOnlyToItsDefaultEndpoint`,\n  `TestShellProviderKeyIsNotSentToACustomEndpoint`,\n  `TestNamedCredentialAndRoleKeyOnACustomEndpoint`), asserted on the\n  `Authorization` header the fake server received.\n\n### Providers and families (module seam)\n\n- `lookupProvider` returns each built-in by exact name only\n  (`TestLookupBuiltinProvider`); `providerAt` example table\n  (`TestProviderAt`); every registered (endpoint, protocol) pair maps\n  back to its provider (`TestProviderAtIsUnique`).\n- `inferFamily` table (`TestInferFamily`); finder flip selects a named\n  profile, an exact route, refuses a same-family target, and treats slash\n  IDs like bare IDs (`TestResolveFinderFlipSelectsNamedProfile`,\n  `TestResolveFinderFlipSelectsExactAuthorRoute`,\n  `TestResolveFinderFlipRejectsSameFamilyTarget`,\n  `TestResolveFinderFlipTreatsSlashIDsLikeBareIDs`); the CLI flag\n  selects the flip and the environment does not\n  (`TestAuthorModelFlagSelectsTheFinderFlip`,\n  `TestAuthorModelEnvironmentDoesNotFlipTheFinder`). Assert on the\n  chosen profile and on whether a note or warning exists, not on text.\n\n## 6. Go baseline references\n\nAt commit `cd08f10`:\n\n- `internal/config/config.go` -- types, `Find`, `Load`, decoding,\n  normalization, settings validation, session header, `ResolveRole`,\n  credential resolution, overrides, `InferFamily`, finder flip,\n  `ValidateRole`, `validateEndpoint`.\n- `internal/config/provider.go` -- built-in provider registry.\n- `internal/config/starter.go` -- `IsPlaceholderModel`, `SchemaRef`\n  (starter itself is in the doctor/init area).\n- `internal/config/trust.go` -- consumer of `ProviderAt` (trust area).\n- `internal/cli/config.go` -- `projectConfigPath`, `gateConfigPath`,\n  `loadProjectConfig`, `withStoredCredentials`, usage errors.\n- `internal/cli/cli.go` -- defaults (lines 156-171), `configInt`,\n  `flagOrConfigInt`, `flagOrConfigDuration`, `applyRoleFlags`.\n- `internal/cli/gate.go` -- gate defaults.\n- `internal/scope/scope.go` -- `GitRoot`.\n- `roast.schema.json` -- current editor schema and descriptions.\n- Tests: `internal/config/config_test.go`, `credential_test.go`,\n  `provider_test.go`, `schema_test.go`, `stored_credential_test.go`;\n  `internal/cli/project_config_test.go`, `settings_test.go`.\n\n## 7. Resolved\n\n1. Key case: JSON keys match exactly; `\"Version\"` is an unknown field\n   (deliberate change 7, `spec.md`). `null` still means absent.\n2. Integer fields reject non-integer lexemes (`1.0`, `1e3`) by raw token\n   text through jsonc-parser's visitor (cross-cutting rule \"Config\n   numbers\", `spec.md`).\n3. Duplicate keys: last wins, same as Go `encoding/json`.\n4. `confidence_floor` values are validated to 0..1 (deliberate change 20,\n   `spec.md`).\n5. `DescribeCredential`, `IsLoopbackHost`, `DescribeEndpoint` are not\n   ported (deliberate change 23, `spec.md`).\n6. WHATWG `URL` is used; only the empty-host case is pinned to Go.\n7. jsonc-parser runs with `allowTrailingComma: true`; strings with lone\n   surrogates are rejected (\"Bytes and text\", `spec.md`); the area exports\n   `renderProjectSchema():string` for the packaging build.\n","numLines":1285,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1285}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01P9ifTZm3gfDYjihBenHPrD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
